In pursuit of this lofty goal, it is crucial to have a detailed and comprehensive overview of the work that has already been reported. Thus, herein we present a summary and assessment of the development of MOF-based materials for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and conversion as illustrated in Scheme 1 for a generic MOF. We start by providing an overview of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture strategies employed in MOFs with specific focus placed on interactions between the frameworks and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Additionally, the challenges and targets intrinsic to the development of MOFs for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture are discussed. Next, recent advances in the field of MOF-based materials for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion, including heterogeneous transformation into organic products, hydrogenation, photocatalytic reduction, and electrocatalytic reduction, are comprehensively described. Thirdly, we provide insight into the relationship between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capacity of MOF-based materials and their catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion performance. Finally, the existing challenges and future directions in this research field are highlighted.
## 2. MOFs for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture
Since their inception over 20 years ago,93,94 MOFs have been targeted for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and capture applications due to their high porosity and chemical tunability.95 To date, many reports focus on the study of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and increasing the capacity in
MOFs as shown in Table 1. Generally, the interaction(s) between the framework and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbate molecules play a critical role, as increasing the strength of such interactions augments the material’s $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity, especially at low loading pressure. The methods employed to increase these interaction strengths stem from tailoring the frameworks’ chemical and physical properties. This relies on the ability to alter the intrinsic properties of MOFs based on judicious design and synthesis of the framework via de novo processes or by postsynthetic modification. In each case, the goal is to leverage MOFs’ chemical tunabilities and compatibilities to adjust the affinity of the framework for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules. Post-synthetic modification has been widely applied recently as a means of achieving this adjustment with a high degree of precision. In this section, we will explain each strategy in detail and provide examples of their employment.
## 2.1. $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding sites within MOFs
Traditional adsorbent materials including zeolites, silicas, polymers, activated carbons, take up $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in a physisorptive manner through the use of weak interactions between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and the pore. Although MOFs also capture $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ by physisorption, their ultrahigh surface areas give much higher uptake capacities compared to other materials. In addition, the advantage of being able to design MOFs that possess features such as open metal sites (OMSs), Lewis basic sites (LBSs), covalently-bound polar functional groups, tunable pore sizes, framework flexibility, and hydrophobicity, provides for a better handle on their tailorability. Specifically, the interaction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with these sites tends to result in excellent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and/or preferential $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption over other gas molecules, such as ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}.$ Needless to say, MOFs can employ multiple interactions in a single structure to achieve extraordinary $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.
2.1.1. Open metal sites (OMSs). Accessing OMSs on MOFs’ multimetallic secondary building units (SBUs) is one of the most widely used strategies to improve their $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture properties. In general, OMSs are generated by treating a MOF with coordinated terminal ligands such as water, $_{N,N}$ -dimethyl formamide (DMF), methanol, on the SBUs, under heat and/or vacuum to remove such ligands with retention of the overall structure. This often results in improved porosity in the activated material. These OMSs commonly serve as Lewis acid sites (LASs) that have been shown to coordinate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Such behavior at Lewis acidic OMSs endows many MOFs with high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ affinities, especially at low adsorbate partial pressures.
The concept of designing OMSs and its implementation was reported in 1998 and 2000.96,97 In 2005, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of HKUST-1, a MOF with OMSs, was found to be about $10\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 42 bar and ambient temperature.98 In HKUST-1, the roomtemperature $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm appears as a typical Type I isotherm in which the most gas is adsorbed at low pressures, leading to a ‘‘knee’’ shape, indicative of high affinity of this MOF for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at low partial pressures. These conditions are interesting with respect to practical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption from gas streams as the material will have large $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture working capacity. Following this work, a wide variety of MOFs bearing OMSs have been applied as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ absorbents, including NU-1000, MIL-101, and M-MOF-74.99–118 Among these, the M-MOF-74 $\mathbf{\left(M\right)}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Co}^{2+},\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}^{2+})$ isoreticular series, composed of an array of one-dimensional hexagonal channels with OMSs at the SBUs, has become one of the most wellstudied sets of MOFs for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture due to their extraordinary $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities at low pressures.110,119 More importantly, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption of this series can be tuned based on the metal from which the MOF is constructed. The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of Mg-MOF-74 (5.4 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 0.1 atm) is 2–4 times higher than those of the other isoreticular M-MOF-74 compounds at the same $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading pressures.120 The zero-coverage isosteric heats of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ of the frameworks details important information regarding the affinity of frameworks for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Applying this analysis to the M-MOF-74 isoreticular series, one concludes that Mg-MOF-74 exhibits the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ affinity $(47\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , whereas Zn-MOF-74, Ni-MOF-74 and Co-MOF-74 exhibit initial affinities of 26, 37, and $41\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , respectively.120 This high affinity for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in Mg-MOF-74 stems from the increased ionic character of the $\mathbf{Mg-O}$ bond that imparts stronger chargequadrupole interactions between the $\mathbf{Mg}^{}$ OMSs and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .
To further understand the mechanism of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on MOFs’ OMSs, density functional theory (DFT) calculations, neutron powder diffraction experiments, infrared and Raman spectroscopy, and in situ solid-state $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) measurements were performed.119,121–123 Generally, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules interact with OMS in an end-on fashion through lone pair electron donation.119 These fairly strong interactions only marginally alter the intramolecular angles of adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Additionally, DFT calculations suggest that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on the OMSs in these materials is still largely physisorptive.121 This not only reduces the energy penalty for adsorbent regeneration, a key consideration in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture, but also accelerates the adsorptive kinetics in MOFs.
With these studies, MOFs were evaluated for potential application in selective adsorption processes. For example, temperature swing post-combustion $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was studied in MOF-177 and Mg-MOF-74.124 While MOF-177 exhibits only poor to modest selectivity, Mg-MOF-74 was marked by tremendous selectivity with an ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) selectivity factor of 148.1 at a typical flue gas temperature $(\sim50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . In fact, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ selectivity of Mg-MOF-74 was even greater than that of the previously most studied zeolite NaX (87.4) under the same conditions. The methods in this study were applied to five different MOFs, targeting pre-combustion $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture, namely the selective capture of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the presence of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ .125 Mg-MOF-74 has the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}/\mathbf{H}_{2}$ selectivity (859 at 5 bar) measured from an $80:20~\mathrm{\H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ mixture at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ because of the greater polarizability of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ compared to $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and the high concentration of OMSs in the MOF pore.
2.1.2. Lewis basic sites (LBSs). While the use of SBU-based OMSs for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture lead to promising results with respect to adsorption capacity and selectivity, another important component of MOFs, the organic linkers, can also augment these properties. For instance, incorporation of organic linkers bearing LBSs into MOFs has been shown to greatly enhance their $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding affinities. This strategy was inspired by the outstanding $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities of aqueous amine solutions such as industrial solutions of monoethanolamine (MEA).126,127 As an attempt to include Lewis basic amines into a MOF, 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\left(\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}–\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C}\right)$ , was utilized in the synthesis of IRMOF-3, a MOF isoreticular to MOF-5.98 At $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , and over a wide pressure range (from 0 to 12.3 bar), IRMOF-3 exhibits a higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity $\left(0{-}14.7\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}\right)$ than MOF-5 $\left(0{-}14.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)\nonumber$ . This study indicated that greater $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ affinities can be achieved by introducing LBSs on the organic linkers. Similar phenomena were also observed in many isoreticular MOFs, such as ${\bf N H}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–53$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66, and ${\bf N H}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125$ .128–131 It is important to note here that many aromatic amines that have been incorporated into MOFs do not necessarily capture $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the same chemisorptive manner as aqueous amine solutions, given that aromatic amines lack the nucleophilicity of the alkylamines in solution. Recently, several functionalized IRMOF-74-III compounds were synthesized in hopes of capturing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the presence of water (Fig. 1).132,133 The linker-based amine functional groups of IRMOF-74-III- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ endowed the material with the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity (3.2 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 800 Torr) of the series. In contrast to the aromatic amine cases detailed above, the alkylamines in IRMOF-74-III- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ chemically bind $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ forming new, covalent species in the MOF. Solid-state crosspolarization magic angle spinning (CP-MAS) $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra indicated that the major chemisorption product in this material was a mixture of carbamic acid (RNHCOOH) and ammonium carbamate $\left(\mathrm{RNHCOO^{-}\Sigma^{+}H_{3}N R}\right)$ . More importantly, since the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture took place at the linker-based alkylamines instead of the OMSs, the influence of water was negligible with respect to the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance of IRMOF-74-III- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Therefore, this MOF captured $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with a high capacity and selectivity even under $65\%$ relative humidity. Recently, this strategy has been improved by doubling the linker-based alkylamines in the framework, yielding IRMOF-74-III- $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)_{2}$ .132 With two alkylamines per organic linker, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the framework increased by more than $100\%$ because the arrangement of the amines in the structure allowed for the formation of only carbamic acids under dry conditions. These carbamic acids are less stable and thus more easily removed, by vacuum or lower heat, than their ammonium carbamate counterparts which are formed when $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ is captured under humid conditions (Fig. 1b). Clearly, the strategy of amine-incorporation onto MOF linkers can be improved to target particular chemisorption products with desirable properties for specific $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture applications.
Apart from amine-functionalized linkers, other nitrogenous linkers, including pyrimidines, triazines, and azoles, have also been utilized to synthesize MOFs bearing LBSs.134–148 In 2012, a new rht-type MOF, Cu-TDPAT, based on an LBS-rich hexacarboxylate ligand $\left(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{TDPAT}\right)$ was reported.135 Its $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity $\left[5.9\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}\right.$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, 10.1 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $273{\bf K}$ and 1 atm) and isosteric heat of adsorption $(42\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ 1 were substantially higher than many previously reported rht-type MOFs. To investigate the interactions between Cu-TDPAT and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ room-temperature IR spectroscopy was conducted after $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading showing the evolution of a distinct band at $\nu=2335\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ perhaps attributable to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at the LBSs.
While LBS incorporation strategies are effective, $\mathbf{MOF-CO}_{2}$ interactions remain mainly single site in nature. Reliance on such interactions can result in relative weak framework affinities for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ or low reversibility.141 As an alternative approach, MOFs can be designed such that their pores are decorated with multiple LBSs. The cooperation between these can endow the resultant MOFs with high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding affinities, high selectivities, and reversible $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. A flexible framework, MAF-23, was made from a chelating-bridging ligand (bis(5-methyl-1H-1,2,4-triazol-3-yl)methane, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{tm})$ ,141 where the available triazolate N-donors of the organic linker acted as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ chelation sites. At 1 atm, MAF-23 exhibited a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of 3.3 and 2.5 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 2a), with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities calculated by Henry’s law of 163 and 107 at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively. To elucidate the dynamic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption behavior, the crystal structure of MAF-23 with different $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading levels was determined using single-crystal X-ray diffraction. From the data, two independent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules could be observed, located near the chelation sites of the organic linkers (Fig. 2b).
The quantity and accessibility of MOFs’ LBSs also play vital roles in the tunability of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities in MOFs. A series of isoreticular copper-based MOFs, ZJNU-43, ZJNU-44 and ZJNU-45, based on three rigid organic likers, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(quinoline-5,8-diyl)- diisophthalate, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(isoquinoline-5,8-diyl)-diisophthalate, and $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(quinoxaline-5,8-diyl)-diisophthalate, were constructed.143 Although these MOFs had nearly identical surface areas, distinct differences were observed in their $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities. In ZJNU-43, the linker-based nitrogen atom is located at the $\mathfrak{a}$ -position in the spacer of the organic linkers. Upon activation, ZJNU-43 adsorbed $\mathrm{4.6~mmol~g^{-1}}{\bf C}{\bf O}_{2}$ at 1 atm and $296\mathrm{K}$ . When the LBSs were shifted to the $\upbeta$ -position in ZJNU-44, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity increased significantly (to $5.2\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ) under the same conditions. When the number of the LBS at the $\mathfrak{a}$ -position was doubled, the adsorption capacity of the resulting MOF, ZJNU-45, was only $4.8\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , slightly greater than ZJNU-43. Quantum chemical calculations indicated that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the structures of ZJNU-43 and ZJNU-45 were trapped in narrow windows, stabilized by numerous weak hydrogen bonds between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the H, N, and O atoms of the organic linkers. In contrast, the LBSs of ZJNU-44 pointed into the pores, allowing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to bridge the organic linkers. Ultimately, the calculated binding energies of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the series of MOFs indicated the value of ZJNU-44 $(-27.6\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})$ was much larger than those of other two MOFs (18.1 and $-18.7\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ), explaining the high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of ZJNU-44. These examples highlight the role of LBSs’ orientation in MOF pores and its impact on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture.
2.1.3. Linkers with polar functional groups. With respect to MOFs synthesized with linkers lacking LBSs, the introduction of alternative polar functional groups such as $-\mathbf{F}$ , –Br, –Cl, –OH, –COOH, ${\bf-N O}_{2}$ , and $-\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ on the organic linkers of MOFs can also enhance $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacity. This is based on interactions between the dipole of the polar functional group and the quadrupole of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Generally, two methods have been employed toward the synthesis of MOFs bearing such polar functional groups. The first is direct synthesis of MOFs using functionalized linkers. Numerous studies indicate that incorporation of these linkers into the frameworks results in favorable $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.149–152 A representative example is found in the synthesis and study of CD-MOF-2, constructed from $\gamma$ cyclodextrin $\left(\gamma\mathbf{-CD}\right)$ and rubidium hydroxide.153 Solid-state NMR experiments showed that the free hydroxyl groups located on $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ reacted with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , forming carbonic acid groups in a chemisorptive fashion (Fig. 3a). This chemisorption led to a large $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity $\left(\sim1.0\mathrm{mmolg}^{-1}\right)$ 1 in the low-pressure region ( $\scriptstyle\left\lfloor<1\right\rfloor$ Torr) between 273–298 K.149 Unfortunately, when the crystals of CD-MOF-2 were pulverized into amorphous powders, very little $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was absorbed and the steep rise also disappeared from the low-pressure region of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms (Fig. 3b and c). This result highlighted the need for crystallinity and porosity in MOFs when targeting gas adsorption.
Another strategy employed to introduce polar functional groups in the pores of MOFs relies on deviating from the commonplace metal-carboxylate chemistry employed in MOF synthesis. To this end, sufficient evidence exists to suggest that organosulfonates may offer a better platform for the construction of MOFs with highly polar pore environments. A mixed-linker strategy was used to synthesize a sulfonate-based MOF $\left(\mathrm{TMOF-1}\right)^{150}$ where the free oxygen atoms from the sulfonate groups were charged and highly polar. Therefore, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of TMOF-1 at $200{-}308\ \mathrm{K}$ was $1.2{-}6.8\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under 1 bar with a high zerocoverage isosteric heat of adsorption of $30.9\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , much higher than many reported organosulfonate-based MOFs. The hypothesized $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ ‘‘encapsulation’’ in which adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was surrounded by four sulfonate groups facilitated the strong $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding in the framework. Polar functional groups can also be incorporated during the synthesis of the extended structure.
A zinc-imidazolate-4-amide-5-imidate framework (IMOF) was synthesized by an in situ ligand reaction and studied for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake.151 During the MOF synthesis, a new, chelating, imidazolate ligand was generated by partial hydrolysis of the original linker, eventually forming narrow channels with polar walls. The total $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the resultant IMOF was $2.1\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, due to the strong interactions between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the amide and imidate functional groups.
The designability and flexibility of MOFs can also be leveraged to introduce polar functional groups into the pore structure.154–161 Utilizing the isoreticular approach, a given MOF’s pore surface functionality can be adjusted by utilizing different organic linkers, all while maintaining the underlying topology of the parent MOF. For example, seven zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), all with GME topology and variously controlled pore metrics and functionality were examined for their $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake behavior.154 The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities of these ZIFs tracked with the expected attraction between polar functional groups in the ZIFs and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . ZIF-78, bearing ${\bf-N O}_{2}$ groups, adsorbed $\sim60~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 800 Torr, a value much higher than the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake values of the other ZIFs in the series with –Cl, –CN, –Me, and –Br moieties. Another example is the stable Al-based MOF, USTC-253, constructed from ${\bf A l}^{3+}$ and dibenzo $[b,d]$ thiophene-3,7- dicarboxylate 5,5-dioxide (Sbpdc) whose structure is isoreticular to MOF-253.155 However, in comparing the total $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities of these two frameworks, the impact of the presence of the linker-based polar sulfone groups becomes apparent. The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of USTC-253 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar is 3.7 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $71\%$ higher than that of MOF-253, despite the presence of bipyridine $\mathbf{N}$ atoms (LBSs) in the latter MOF. Moreover, when trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) was utilized as a modulator during the synthesis of USTC-253, the resultant USTC-253-TFA exhibited a higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity due to the introduction of defects. Additionally, the framework adsorbed very little ${\bf N}_{2}$ under the same conditions, yielding a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 75 : 1. Such results highlight the importance of polar functional group incorporation in MOFs and the immense potential of application of this strategy to post-combustion $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture.
2.1.4. Pore size control. $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ has a relatively small kinetic diameter (3.3 Å). For this reason, micropores are typically more advantageous than mesopores and/or macropores when targeting enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and selectivity. In the context of the application of MOFs to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture, deliberate selection of metal salts and organic linkers can result in the formation of a variety of pore sizes. For example, by altering the length of the organic linkers in two frameworks, isoreticular series of MOF-5 and MOF-74 with pore apertures ranging from 3.8 to $28.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and from 14 to $98{\mathring{\mathbf{A}}}$ , respectively, were prepared.162,163 With this as a backdrop, an ultra-microporous (3.5 and 4 Å) MOF, Ni-4PyC, based on a short ligand 4-pyridylcarboxylate (4Pyc) was reported.164 Grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations predicted that Ni-4PyC could adsorb up to 8.2 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at 10 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ .164 The experimental $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption measurement result ( $\mathrm{~\stackrel{~}{~}~}\sim8.2\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 10 bar and 298 K) was in good agreement with the simulated isotherm. In addition, simulations indicated that three main $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding locations existed in Ni-4PyC, located in the intersections of channels, 1D channels, and spherical cages. Also, cooperative binding between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules was proposed to play a significant role in the exceptionally high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of Ni-4PyC. Unexpectedly, in contrast to the excellent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance, Ni-4PyC adsorbed almost no $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ even at very high pressure (up to 35 bar), making it an ideal candidate for pre-combustion $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture. Using a similar strategy, a microporous MOF, UTSA-16, assembled from $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ citric acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}c i t)}$ , and KOH was prepared and studied by neutron diffraction to reveal two pairs of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules trapped by hydrogen-bonding in the materials’ micro pores $(\sim3.3\times5.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2})$ (Fig. 4).165 These small diamond cages and binding sites not only endowed UTSA-16 with a significantly large $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity $(160~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $296\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar) but also enhanced the MOF’s $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (29.8 at $200~\mathrm{{kPa}}$ and 296 K) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (314.7 at $100~\mathrm{{kPa}}$ and 296 K) separation capabilities. As an example contributing to the fundamental understanding of precise $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding modes in a given MOF, the study on UTSA-16 demonstrates that measuring capacity alone may not be sufficient to address underlying framework chemistry.
Self-interpenetration resulting in mutually interlocking frameworks is known to occur in certain MOF topologies.166 This has a couple of effects on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture: when interpenetration reduces surface area, this in turn reduces the overall capacity; however, when more restrictive pores result from interpenetration, it has the effect of increasing the binding energy of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and therefore giving more favorable uptake.166 There are cases where interpenetration as well as mixed linkers in MOFs produce what has been called pore space partition (PSP), where the original pores have been segmented in a designed fashion. This also has the effect of restricting pores and increasing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake.166,167 A 2-fold interpenetrated microporous MOF, $\mathrm{Cu(FMA)}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPE})_{0.5},$ with a $3.6\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ pore diameter was reported and exhibited highly selective adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at 195 K.168 Two nearly identical frameworks, SNU-70 and SNU-71, in which the only difference between the organic linkers was the discrepancy between a $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ or a C–C bond.169 SNU-71, which is interpenetrated, exhibited high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities at low pressures regardless of temperature, due to the decreased pore size compared to the non-interpenetrated SNU-70. In SUMOF- $X\left(X=2\mathrm{-}4\right)$ , $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{BDC}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{NDC}$ , and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{BPDC}$ were utilized as linkers to obtain three interpenetrated MOFs.170 The authors speculated that the higher uptake of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in these interpenetrated MOFs compared with their non-interpenetrated counterparts could be attributed to the increased electric field gradients in the small pores of interpenetrated frameworks, enhancing the interactions between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules and the host framework. Apart from structures that commonly interpenetrate, an unusual partially interpenetrated MOF, NOTT-202a, formed as the result of a pronounced framework phase transition during desolvation.171 At $195\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, NOTT-202a exhibited a high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ storage capacity of $20\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ , exceeding many previously reported values in $\mathbf{In}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ -based MOFs.171
Pore space partition (PSP) serves to decrease MOFs’ pore sizes by dividing large cages or channels into smaller segments by the insertion of additional organic linkers or other guest species.79 PSP has gradually became a crucial tool to efficiently utilize the pore spaces of MOFs.79,172 A microporous anionic framework, $[\mathrm{In}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{4}]_{n}^{3n-}$ , was synthesized in seven distinct chemical environments, including a variety of organic solvents, ionic liquids, and deep eutectic solvents.173 Some of the solvents were incorporated into the resultant MOFs as extraframework charge-balancing species. Even with the occupation of a large percentage of the pore space by charge-balancing cations like choline, the MOF, (choline) $_3[\mathrm{In}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{4}]$ 2DMF, exhibited a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $3.2\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 atm and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ on account of reasonably partitioned pore spaces and hydroxyl groups on the choline cations. The same group reported an interesting cage-within-cage indium-based MOF (CPM-5) by employing $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ and $\mathbf{BTC}^{3-}$ as metal ions and linkers, respectively.174 In the structure of CPM-5, a small Archimedean $\mathbf{In}_{12}$ cage is encapsulated within a large Archimedean $\mathbf{In}_{24}$ cage and every two adjacent $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ sites of the $\mathrm{In}_{24}$ cage are connected by two carboxylate groups from the $\mathbf{BTC}^{3-}$ linker. The remaining carboxylate group from $\mathbf{BTC}^{3-}$ connects the outer $\mathrm{In}_{24}$ cage with the inner $\mathbf{In}_{12}$ cage. At $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, CPM-5 adsorbed a significant amount of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ $\left(3.6\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ . This was attributed to the synergy between open $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ sites, charges in the framework, and the pore space partitioning effect from the cage-within-cage architecture. More recently, a symmetry-matching regulated ligand insertion strategy was developed to make crystalline porous materials with exceptional $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities.175 To partition the pore space, organic linkers with $C_{3}$ symmetry such as 2,4,6-tri(4-pyridyl)-1,3,5-triazine (tpt) were employed to occupy the channel of MIL-88 type structures. This yielded a series of isoreticular CPM- $\cdot X$ $\mathit{X}=33\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{d}\mathit{\check{\Psi}}_{.}$ ) structures by fragmenting the channels into numerous small segments (Fig. 5). By providing additional adsorptive surfaces upon incorporation of tpt, CPM-33b exhibited a very high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity (7.8 and $5.6~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively, under 1 bar) and a moderate isosteric heat of adsorption $(25.0\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})$ even through the OMSs in the structure were coordinated by tpt ligands.
2.1.5. Flexible frameworks. Another property of MOFs is their propensity for structural flexibility. Upon exposure to external stimuli such as pressure and/or temperature, certain MOFs undergo structural change(s), resulting in ‘‘gated’’ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. In such cases, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of these MOFs suddenly and drastically increase at a definite relative pressure known as a gate pressure. It is generally accepted that the structural flexibility of MOFs is mainly related to the so-called soft porous crystals with a ‘‘breathing effect’’.38,176,177 In a representative example, a flexible, doubly interpenetrated zinc(II) MOF was reported.178 The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm revealed a drastic adsorption step at about 10 bar and significant hysteresis during $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ desorption.178 The calculated $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake and $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ were 7.1 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 30 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $\sim20\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kcal}\ m o l^{-1}}$ , respectively. Variable temperature and pressure PXRD measurements were performed to study the effect of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on the underlying MOF structure. The results indicated that the framework contracted upon activation accompanied by considerable changes in the powder pattern. When $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was loaded into the framework, the original pattern reappeared, indicating that a reversible structural transformation or ‘‘breathing’’ behavior was occurring. Another interesting example of the application of ‘‘breathing’’ MOFs to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture can be found in a series of flexible pillared-layered MOFs. Based on a library of variably functionalized bdc-type linkers, the dangling side chains of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]_{n}$ acted as immobilized ‘‘guests’’ and interacted with other guest molecules, themselves, and the framework backbone.179 This had the effect of significantly enhancing the frameworks’ flexibility. Gas sorption measurements showed that each MOF could selectively adsorb $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in a distinct stepwise fashion, while ${\bf N}_{2}$ was almost never adsorbed in any appreciable quantity. More recently, SHF-61, a diamondoid continuous-breathing network, was synthesized from reactions between $\mathrm{InCl}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC.180 Desolvation of the as-synthesized material led to a drastic change in not only the MOF’s morphology but also its unit-cell volume $(\Delta V\approx2000\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ ), as monitored by a series of single-crystal X-ray diffraction experiments. The partially solvated SHF-61-DMF exhibited the largest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake among those in the series $\left(2.4~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ at 20 bar and 298 K) with minor desorption hysteresis caused by a structural phase transition. An elastic layered MOF, ELM-11, displayed a gate-adsorption isotherm (195 K) with two steps at $0.2\mathrm{-}0.4\ \mathrm{kPa}$ and $14{-}30\mathrm{kPa}$ .181 With the aid of in situ synchrotron PXRD measurements, two structural transitions and one gradual deformation of ELM-11 were observed during the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption process.181
Structural flexibility can also stem from the incorporation of organic linkers into MOFs that are sensitive to external stimuli such as light.182,183 As an example of such behavior, we turn to a photoswitchable MOF (PCN-123) made from $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(NO}_{3}\mathrm{)}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 2-(phenyldiazenyl)terephthalate which is isostructural to MOF-5.182 UV irradiation and heat treatment isomerized the organic linkers in trans-to-cis and cis-to-trans fashions, respectively. Based on this isomerization, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of PCN-123 could be reversibly altered with the all trans configuration of the organic linkers yielding the higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity (Fig. 6). An example of low-energy $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and release in a light-responsive framework, $\mathrm{Zn(AzDC)(4,4^{\prime}–B P E)_{0.5}}$ ， was also reported.183 When the AzDC and $^{4,4^{\prime}-\mathrm{BPE}}$ linkers were photoisomerized between cis and trans states upon exposure to $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ light, an instantaneous release of about $64\%$ (under dynamic measurements) and $42\%$ (under static conditions) of adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was observed. It is important to note that significant structural change in $\mathrm{Zn(AzDC)(4,4^{\prime}–B P E)_{0.5}}$ was not observed by synchrotron X-ray diffraction.
As has previously been discussed, gas separation and purification can be carried out by tailoring porous materials to have specific interactions with the molecule of interest. Therefore, discriminatory gate-opening can endow flexible MOFs with highly efficient $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ separation properties.184–189 For instance, one can observe conventional $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake behavior with dynamic adsorption of other species. Such a phenomenon was reported for a flexible porous coordination polymer (PCP), $[\mathbf{Mn}(\mathbf{bdc})(\mathbf{dpe})],$ with zero-dimensional pores.187 In addition to the Type I $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm (at 195 K), $\mathbf{Mn(bdc)(dpe)}$ displayed gate opening adsorption behavior for acetylene $\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)$ . Below the gate opening pressure $\left(P_{\mathrm{{go}}}=1.45\mathrm{{kPa}}\right)$ , negligible $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ was adsorbed by the PCP, endowing the material with highly selective adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . As expected, IAST calculations indicated that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity ranged from 8.8 to 13 for an equimolar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas mixture. To investigate the mechanism of selective adsorption, $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{dpe})$ was postsynthetically modified by $[2+2]$ photodimerization of the dpe linkers yielding a new PCP, $\mathbf{Mn}_{2}(\mathbf{bdc})_{2}$ (rctttpcb). In the new PCP, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacities were nearly identical $\mathrm{0.6~mmol~g^{-1}}$ vs. $0.5\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}},$ , supporting the hypothesis that interactions between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the linker-based phenylene rings played crucial roles in the gradual $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in $\mathbf{Mn(bdc)(dpe)}$ . It was also found that the chain length of alkyl ether groups (dangling on the benzene rings) and group substitution pattern of organic linkers had huge impacts on the structural flexibility and adsorption selectivity of MOFs.188 $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{L}^{1})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})$ ${(\mathrm{L}^{1}=2,5}$ -bis(2-methoxyethoxy)benzene), which is isoreticular to $\mathtt{\left[Z n_{2}(b d c)_{2}.(d a b c o)\right]}_{n}$ ,190 exhibits a unique twostep $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake isotherm due to its structural flexibility. However, negligible $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption was observed at 77 and $195\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively. The authors speculated that the flexible and polar alkyl ether groups served as molecular gates at the MOF’s pore apertures. Therefore, polar gas molecules such as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ could penetrate the molecular gates more easily than their nonpolar counterparts such as ${\bf N}_{2}$ .
2.1.6. Hydrophobic frameworks. It is important to recognize that all practical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption must occur in the presence of water. Therefore, hydrophobic frameworks are suitable for capturing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ under humid conditions. Although water’s large dipole moment causes it to interact more strongly than $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with most frameworks, the hydrophobicity of certain MOFs allows them to suppress competitive water adsorption.
Generally, the hydrophobicity of a given MOF stems from bulky hydrophobic organic linkers or pendant hydrophobic groups on the organic linkers. For example, a series of ZIFs of chabazite (CHA) zeolite topology were made from a mixture of linkers.191 The entire series displayed negligible water uptake and were able to separate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ from mixed gas streams without any loss of uptake capacity over three cycles under dry or humid conditions. Additionally, hydrophobic MOFs can employ the strategies we have previously outlined. For example, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties of a flexible and superhydrophobic MOF, FMOF-1, were modeled under humid conditions using quantum mechanical simulations.192 With many $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ groups lining the pores, FMOF-1 exhibited superhydrophobicity as indicated by its high contact angle with water $(158^{\circ})$ . Simulated mixed-gas isotherms of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and water demonstrated that the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of FMOF-1 was not influenced by water even at $80\%$ relative humidity. Analogously, a family of isostructural MOFs was made by employing bi-functional organic linkers with both basic azolyl and chelating carboxylate groups.193 The basic azolyl functional groups interacted with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the $\mathbf{-OCH}_{3}$ groups from acetate moieties on the pore surface provided a hydrophobic lining to the pore-walls, allowing the MOF to maintain approximately $80\%$ of its $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity upon exposure to $75\%$ relative humidity.
## 2.2. Crafting MOFs’ interiors through post-synthetic modification
The intrinsic features of MOFs provide favorable conditions for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation. Nevertheless, in the de novo synthesis of MOFs, the introduction of certain specific adsorption sites or functional groups can be challenging due to poor solubility of the organic linkers, competitive reactions between functional groups and the target frameworks, or the sensitivity of frameworks to the alteration of either the metal ions/ligands or their coordination environment(s). In this context, post-synthetic strategies for the incorporation of desired functionalities into MOFs have been developed. In general, post-synthetic approaches to improve MOFs’ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capabilities can be classified into two broad categories: post-synthetic modification, and the formation of MOF composites.
2.2.1. Post-synthetic modification. Post-synthetic modification (PSM) can serve as a facile technique to introduce new $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorptive sites into MOFs without significantly altering or damaging the underlying structure.194 Specifically, PSM has been employed to accomplish the following: (1) introduction of additional functional groups, such as amine groups, or exchange of metals in the SBUs, (2) modification, insertion, or exchange of organic linkers, and (3) cation exchange in anionic MOFs.
In the case of SBU PSM, grafting alkylamine moieties onto OMSs serves as an effective means of improving the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake performances of MOFs based on the aforementioned strong affinities of alkylamines for Lewis acidic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules. As an example, the coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ sites in a water stable and trizolate-bridged MOF (Cu-BTT) were capped with ethylenediamine (en).106 Although the pores of resultant material were occluded leading to relatively low $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake at relatively high pressures, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake at low pressures, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity, and $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values dramatically increased compared to those associated with the pristine MOF. This technique was also successfully extended to other MOF systems.99,195–204 Among these, Mg-MOF-74, with a high concentration of OMSs and one of the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities at low $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressures $\left[6.1\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.15 bar),120 has been identified as an ideal platform for post-synthetic amine functionalization. Unfortunately, its relatively narrow, one-dimensional channels $(\sim11\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}})$ hinder effective diffusion of sterically bulky amines into the pores. For this reason, extended MOF-74-type structures such as $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dotpdc})$ and $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dondc})$ were utilized as substitutes for post-synthetic amine incorporation. All of the diamineappended MOFs exhibited not only exceptional $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities but also high CO2 adsorption selectivity.99,196–198,201–204
Additionally, the thermal and chemical stabilities under humid condition of the resultant materials were markedly enhanced, despite reduction of their Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas and pore volumes.99,198–200 As an alternative to poreexpansion, the OMSs of Mg-MOF-74 were capped with a much smaller amine (hydrazine), yielding $\mathbf{Mg-MOF-74}\big(\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4}\big)_{1.8}$ .199 The authors assumed that hydrazine would promote effective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in the MOF after PSM due to its small size, yet similar chemistry to other amines. Additionally, the distances between two adjacent amines were quite large. This inhibited the formation of weak hydrogen bonds and greatly enhanced the utility and availability of the grafted amines. Ultimately, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $\mathbf{Mg-MOF-}74\big(\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4}\big)_{1.8}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.4 mbar, the partial pressure of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in air, was $3.9\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ due to the high concentration $\left(6.0\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}\right)$ of surface-appended hydrazines. The $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ calculated by the Clausius–Clapeyron equation and the Virial fitting method gave near zero-coverage values of 118 and $90\mathrm{~kJ~mol}^{-1}$ , respectively. These high heats of adsorption necessitate relatively high regeneration temperatures for such adsorbents. This interplay is an outstanding challenge in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture; one desires strong chemical bonds between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules and the adsorbent to achieve selectivity, but strong bonds also typically require high adsorbent regeneration temperatures. As such, investigations into the modification of MOF SBUs with other $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -philic moieties were carried out. For example, a new functionalization technique, solvent assisted ligand incorporation (SALI) was developed with the goal of introducing perfluorinated chains onto the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ SBU of NU-1000. In this case, the technique relied on acid–base chemistry between the hydroxyl groups on the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ SBU and the carboxylate group of the fluoroalkyl linker (Fig. 7).205 All the fluoroalkanefunctionalized samples had higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities and zero-uptake limit $(Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0})$ than those of the parent NU-1000, which can be attributed to the interactions between the $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{F}$ dipoles and the quadrupole of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . More importantly, the $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value for each sample was no greater than $35\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , implying that these adsorbents could be easily regenerated.
Post-synthetic modification for enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture in MOFs is not limited to the modification of the metals’ coordination environments. Post-synthetic exchange (PSE) of metal atoms in SBUs is also a promising approach and can improve the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capabilities and/or selectivities of MOFs.
The $\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{{\left(rv\right)}}$ ions of UiO-66 can be exchanged with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{w})$ ions by immersing UiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr_{100}})$ in a DMF solution of ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{4}({\mathrm{THF}})_{2}$ for various lengths of time at $368\mathrm{~K~}$ to give UiO- ${\bf\cdot}66(\bf{Z r/T i})$ with progressively higher Ti loadings.206 The pore size distributions of the resultant frameworks as measured by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption experiments demonstrated that the size of the octahedral pores dramatically decreased as a result of the shorter Ti–O bonds compared to $\mathbf{\Delta}_{\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{O}}$ bonds. The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr/Ti})$ increased from 2.2 $\left(\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}_{100})\right)$ to $4.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $\left(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Ti}_{56})\right)$ , which was ascribed to synergistic effects between the reduced pore size and framework density of the Ti-exchanged UiO-66. The highest $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ value of the frameworks in this report was only about $38\mathrm{~kJ~mol}^{-1}$ , significantly lower than those of most amine-decorated MOFs. Though not mentioned in the original report, the working capacity of such materials might improve as their heats of adsorption are lower, but it comes at the cost of diminished framework $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacity. In order to stoichiometrically load new metal cations in MOFs, a new PSM strategy to exchange metal ions in a chromium-based MOF, porph@MOM-11 was devised. In porph@MOM-11, cationic porphyrin linkers serve as the pore walls.207 Simple immersion of porph@MOM-11 into methanol (MeOH) solutions of different metal chlorides gave four PSM derivatives with different metal site occupancies. Due to the reduced pore sizes and the introduction of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ and metal ions, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ volumetric uptake capacities (at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm) and $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values of each of the PSM variants were improved by $3.1\substack{-14.5\%}$ and $6.6\substack{-36.0\%}$ , respectively, in comparison to those of porph $\textcircled{2}$ MOM-11.
Apart from modification and exchange of metal centers, modification, insertion and exchange of the organic linkers in MOFs can also enhance the interactions between frameworks and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Organic linkers can be post-synthetically modified to introduce diverse $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -philic moieties.208–210 For example, alkylamines were tethered to $\mathbf{Cr-MIL-}101{\cdot}\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ through a simple Brønsted acid–base reaction.208 The resultant Cr-MIL-101- $\mathrm{so}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ -TAEA (TAEA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tris(2-aminoethyl)amine) had a high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of 2.3 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 150 mbar and $313\mathrm{~K~}$ , and 1.1 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.4 mbar and $293{\mathrm{~K}}$ The $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ value as calculated from a triple-site Langmuir model was $87\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at zero coverage, indicating strong chemisorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{Cr-MIL-}101{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}.$ -TAEA. UiO-66-allyl was also subjected to a plethora of post-synthetic functionalization reactions.209 Amongst the modified UiO-66 analogues, the amino alcohol-decorated MOF (UiO-66-aminoalcohol) exhibited the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity $\left(11.6~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ at $308\mathrm{~k~}$ and 20 bar), while only a negligible amount of ${\bf N}_{2}$ was adsorbed under the same conditions, suggesting that UiO-66-aminoalcohol could be a promising candidate for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas separation.
Insertion of organic linkers emerged as the second PSM technique with a goal of incorporating new organic linkers of similar or identical length, but bearing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -phillic moieties in MOFs.211,212 A bridging linker was post-synthetically incorporated into a MOF by a single crystal to single crystal (SCSC) transformation.211 By inserting a 3,6-di(4-pyridyl)-1,2,4,5- tetrazine (bpta) linker into the zinc-based MOF, SNU-30, the honeycomb-like pores were divided into smaller channels along the ab plane of the framework, yielding SNU-31. This PSM slightly decreased the framework’s void volume from $82.1\%$ to $76.1\%$ . Gas adsorption isotherms showed that ${\bf N}_{2}.$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacity of SNU-30 could reach up to $7.04\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ( $77\mathrm{K}.$ 1 bar), 16.2 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (77 K, 61 bar), 0.23 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ ( $298\ \mathrm{K},$ 1 bar), and 1.16 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (298 K, 1 bar), respectively. In contrast to SNU-30, SNU-31 displayed almost no adsorption capacity for ${\bf N}_{2},\ {\bf H}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , whereas it exhibited moderate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake $\mathrm{\Delta}0.6\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ at 1 bar) at $298{\mathrm{~K}}.$ . Another interesting example was found in the incorporation of five distinct linkers into a flexible MOF using a post-synthetic variable-spacer installation strategy (Fig. 8).212 Unlike the effects observed in rigid frameworks, the structural flexibility of LIFM-28 enabled the material’s pore size to be finely tuned by the insertion of organic linkers with various lengths and substituents.212 The resultant MOFs had functionalized pore environments, and improved thermal stabilities and BET surface areas. Due to these alterations, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and selectivity were greatly enhanced. The $\left.-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right.$ groups in LIFM-33 endowed it with the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity $\left(3.6{\mathrm{~mmol~g}}^{-1}\right)$ with a high $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ of about $40\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ based on the presence of strong ${\bf N H}_{2}–{\bf C O}_{2}$ interactions. The IAST calculation revealed that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption selectivity in LIFM-33 was about 30, demonstrably higher than that of parent MOF.
Linker exchange can also regulate MOFs’ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture performance while maintaining their crystallinity, stability, and porosity.213–215 A family of water-stable UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{AD}}_{n}$ frameworks modified by diverse flexible alkanedioic acids $\left(\left(\mathrm{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)_{n-2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{H}\right);$ $n=4$ , 6, 8 and 10) was reported.213 $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra of digested MOFs demonstrated that the degree of linker substitution increased as the hydrocarbon chain in the substituting organic linker was elongated. Each terephthalate ligand of UiO-66 was substituted by two alkanedioic acid. Among the investigated frameworks, UiO-66-AD6 had the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ desorption free energy due to the relatively strong interactions between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the carboxyl groups of the flexible alkanedioic acid linker which was similar in length to the BDC linker of UiO-66. In this case, the linkers could also be replaced by metalated-ligands.214 The exchange process was carried out by incubating UiO-66 in aqueous solutions of 1,2,4,5-benzenetetracarboxylic acid (BTEC) or mellitic acid (MA) and various alkaline hydroxides, including LiOH, NaOH, and KOH. UiO-66- $(\mathsf{C O O N a})_{2}.$ EX exhibited the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}/\mathbf{N}_{2}$ selectivity (33.3) while UiO-66- $\mathrm{(COOLi)_{4}\mathrm{{-EX}}}$ had the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity $\left(0.7\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}\right.$ at 0.15 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ ). The authors attributed these effects to the mutual influence of the cations’ polarizing abilities and reduced pore sizes.
With respect to ionic MOFs, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties can be regulated by cation exchange.216–219 A series of cationexchanged ionic MOFs were prepared by soaking Bio-MOF-1 in DMF solutions of tetramethylammonium, tetraethylammonium, or tetrabutylammonium.216 Despite the observation of decreased BET surface areas and pore volumes in each cation-exchanged MOF, their $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacities and $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values improved compared to the parent Bio-MOF-1. This was attributed to strong interactions between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the newly-inserted ammonium cations. The same team also exchanged the cations of Bio-MOF-1 with a series of guanidinium derivatives.217 When a guanidinium derivative with a high population of LBSs, diaminoguanidinium (DiAmGND+), was exchanged into bio-MOF-1, the MOF’s $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ increased to 5.1 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar) and $29.4~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ , respectively. This was accompanied by only a slight decrease in both the pore volume and BET surface area of DiAmGND@bio-MOF-1. The authors speculated that the enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake performance could be ascribed to the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ affinity of aminated cations rather than the decreased pore size. It was demonstrated that $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ exchange in an anionic MOF, $[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}(\mathrm{Me})_{2}]_{2}[\mathrm{Zn}_{5}(\mathrm{L})_{3}].$ 5.5DMF $3.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathbf{\bar{(L=3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}}$ -biphenyltetracarboxylic acid), can be accomplished by SCSC transformation.218 Impressively, both the guest ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{N}}({\mathrm{Me}})_{2}^{+}$ counterions located in the pores and some coordinated metal ions $(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+})$ of the MOF were replaced by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ , resulting in a greatly increased BET surface area (from 342 to $1664~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity (from 1.7 to $6.6~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar).
2.2.2. MOF composites. Although a demonstrably powerful technique, post-synthetic modification of MOFs requires OMSs, specific functional groups on the organic linkers, and/or sufficiently large pore apertures to allow for guest or reagent diffusion. The question then becomes, how can one improve the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture performance of MOFs lacking these features? An alternative strategy, the formation of MOF composites by accommodating various guest species or combining with other materials such as polymers or ILs can enhance the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capabilities of MOFs lacking such features. The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities of the resultant MOF composites are typically significantly greater than those of the parent MOFs due to the generation of new pore environment(s), additional interactions at the interface between phases, and/or affinity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to the guest(s). Moreover, the plasticity and chemical stability of certain compounds utilized in the formation of MOF composites can lead to enhanced stabilities and mechanical properties of the resultant materials, expanding the range of practical applications. To date, four classes of materials, including small molecules,220–222 polymers,223–226 two-dimensional (2D) materials,227–234 and threedimensional (3D) materials,235–237 have combined with MOFs in order to tune their $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties.
$\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ is highly soluble in many liquid-phase small molecules, including ILs. Therefore, many experiments and computational simulations have been conducted to explore the applicability of IL-MOF composites to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture, separation and purification.220–222,238,239 For example, the theoretical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ separation performance of several room temperature ionic liquid (RTIL)–MOF composites was evaluated. In the study, 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium $\left[\mathrm{EMIM^{+}}\right]$ salts were incorporated in HKUST-1.239 At low pressures, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities, $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities were significantly enhanced upon incorporation of RTILs due to the strong chemisorption of ‘‘dissolution’’ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in such compounds. The successful incorporation of $_{1-n}$ -butyl-3-methylimidazolium hexafluorophosphate $\left([\mathbf{BMM}][\mathbf{PF}_{6}]\right)$ into a zeolitic imidazolate framework, ZIF-8, led to a $\mathrm{\big[BMIM\big]}\mathrm{\big[PF_{6}\big]}/\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ composite.220 The researchers then investigated the composite’s gas adsorption performance using a wide array of experiments, atomically detailed simulations, and DFT calculations. Although the gas adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{\big[BMIM\big]}\mathrm{\big[PF_{6}\big]}/\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ was attenuated compared to the pristine ZIF-8, the selectivities $\mathrm{{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{{CH}_{4}}}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , in $50/50~\mathrm{v/v}$ mixtures) of the composite were more than twice as high than those of the parent ZIF-8 at low pressures. Such work indicated that incorporation of ILs in MOFs can yield materials that exhibit strong, preferential interactions with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .
In the context of MOF composite formation, polymers possess various unique attributes such as softness and high stability. These properties facilitate integration with MOFs. Among them, polyethyleneimine (PEI), a polymer with repeating amine groups and two aliphatic carbon $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathbf{CH}_{2}\right)$ spacers, has been incorporated in MOFs to augment both the strength and number of adsorbent– $\mathbf{\cdotCO}_{2}$ interactions. The numerous amine sites, commercial availability, and low volatility relative to low-molecular weight amines such as monoethanolamine make PEI an ideal candidate for MOF composite formation. A series of PEI-decorated MOFs were prepared with various amounts of PEI using a wet impregnation method.223 In the resultant composites, low molecularweight linear PEI was tethered onto the exposed $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ centers of MIL-101. This left other amine groups available to interact with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . As expected, the BET surface areas and pore volumes of the MOF composites dramatically decreased compared to the parent MIL-101 due to pore occlusion. However, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of the PEI-MIL-101 samples were significantly enhanced between 0–2 bar. When the PEI loading was increased to $100\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of the resultant composite (PEI-MIL-101-100) was over 12 times greater (4.2 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.15 bar) than that of pristine MIL-101. Additionally, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities (in a ${\bf15/75\bf{v/v}}$ mixture) of PEIMIL-101-100 and PEI-MIL-101-125, with 100 and $125~\mathrm{wt\%}$ PEI loading, respectively, at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were as high as 120 and 150, respectively. Given the ease with which small MOF particles can be loaded with PEI and the fact that branched PEI possesses more-nucleophilic primary amines $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ as opposed to the secondary amine groups (–NH–) present in linear PEI, the former was impregnated into MIL-101 crystals about 150 and $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in size.224 Each composite exhibited an exceptionally high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity at 0.15 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . However, the performances of the small MOF composites (with higher PEI loadings) were slightly worse than those of the large one, likely due to inner pore inaccessibility.
Polymers trapped within the channels of MOFs can also serve to partition the pore spaces. Recently, a hydrophobic MOF–polymer composite, PN@MOF-5 ( $\mathrm{\mathop{PN}=}$ polynaphthylene), was fabricated (Fig. 9a).225 As the PN loading increased, the number of $0.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ diameter pores drastically increased with a simultaneous decrease in the amount of $1.2\:\mathrm{nm}$ pores (the pore size of MOF-5). This indicated that the channels of MOF-5 were partitioned by incorporation of the PN polymer. As expected, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake increased from 1.7 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (MOF-5) to $3.5~\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $\left(\mathbf{PN}@\mathbf{MO-}5\right)$ at $273~\mathrm{~K~}$ and 760 Torr. Most importantly, the stability of $\mathbf{PN}@\mathbf{MOF}{\cdot}5$ under humid conditions was significantly greater than that of MOF-5, which is typically unstable in water or humid air. The dynamic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the hydrophobic composite under humid conditions was nearly identical to that of the composite under dry conditions (Fig. 9b).
MOF composites can also be prepared by growing MOFs on the surface or within the pores of other materials. Some 2D materials such as graphene, graphene oxide, layered double hydroxides and aminoclays can be used as substrates for the growth of MOF composites because their surfaces can be functionalized and/or they frequently possess diverse, reactive functional groups. Several benzoic acid-functionalized graphene/M-MOF-74 composites, MCGr-X $\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}\right.$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $X=0$ , 2, 5, and $10~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ), were synthesized in which the graphene basal planes acted as supports to reinforce the MOF matrices (Fig. 10).227 Interestingly, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of each MCGr-X composite was enhanced relative to that of the corresponding pristine MOF. As the most striking example, MgCGr-10 adsorbed 8.1 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at 1 atm and $298\mathrm{K}$ . Since the composite exhibited similar BET surface areas and pore volumes to the pristine MOF, the increased $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and selectivity of the composite were attributed to dispersive forces between the $\pi$ electron cloud of graphene and quadrupole moment of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Moreover, mechanical property measurements such as nanoindentation revealed that both the hardness and elastic modulus of each MOF increased upon graphene introduction, which would undoubtedly be favorable with respect to practical technological application of MOFs and MOF-based materials. Two-dimensional (2D) materials can also act as substrates or templates during the construction of MOF composites. For example, an HKUST-1–graphene oxide composite in which the oxygen-containing functional groups from the graphene oxide (GO) acted as seed sites for crystallization and dispersion of HKUST-1 nanocrystals.228 Interestingly, the defects in the GO layers inhibited the growth of the MOF nanoparticles (NPs). As such, the HKUST-1 crystallites were relatively small $\left(10{-}40\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ . Although GO is not intrinsically porous, the composite exhibited both an augmented porosity (BET surface area, $1532~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake $\mathrm{8.3~mmol~g^{-1}}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm) compared to the original HKUST-1 (BET surface area of $1305~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $6.4{\mathrm{~mmol~g}}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm). The authors attributed this to the improved gas accessibility of the well-dispersed nanoMOF crystallites. Another example of 2D material MOF composite templation was reported in the application of highly waterdispersible aminoclay (AC) sheets to the synthesis of HKUST-1–AC composites.229 The amine functional groups in the AC served as metal binding sites for the stabilization of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions, which lead to decreased aggregation of HKUST-1 crystallites during the growth process. Therefore, the resultant MOF composites were decorated with ultra-small $\left(2{-}3{\mathrm{~nm}}\right)$ HKUST-1 NPs. The BET surface area and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity values for the HKUST-1–AC composite were $1381~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and 8.1 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar), respectively, representing increases of $70\%$ and $39\%$ , respectively, over the reported values for HKUST-1. These enhancements could potentially stem from the formation of new voids at the interface of HKUST-1 NPs and the AC layers. This strategy was adapted to other systems, including aminated graphite oxide,230,231 suggesting its potential universality.
It is important to note, however, that many 2D materials do not exhibit high surface areas. As an alternative, many three dimensional (3D) materials (and even some one dimensional [1D] materials) have moderate to high porosities and can be beneficial for MOF composite formation based on their potential miscibility with MOFs. A representative example is found in the incorporation of carbon nanotubes (CNTs) into HKUST-1 to give a CNT hybrid composite CNT@HKUST-1.240 The carboxylate groups of the CNTs acted as nucleation sites to support continuous growth of HKUST-1. This crystal growth was beneficial for thorough removal of guest molecules during activation, leading to an enhanced composite pore volume $\mathrm{(from~0.73~}$ to $0.87~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ isotherm revealed that the adsorption capacity of CNT@HKUST-1 was $13.5~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 18 bar, about twice as high as that of HKUST-1 under the same conditions. Additionally, crystal growth confinement in the mesopores or macropores of templates can lead to smaller MOF particle sizes than those of MOFs synthesized via the de novo process.235,236 Typically, smaller MOF crystals can be more easily and efficiently activated, which is naturally beneficial with respect to gas adsorption as active sites and pore volumes are more easily accessible in properly evacuated materials. Finally, due to the discrepancies between the pore diameters of MOFs and other materials, the pore size distributions of composite materials can fall within a wide range, thus potentially improving $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and selectivity.241
## 2.3. Challenges and opportunities in developing MOFs for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture
Remarkable advances have been made toward $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation in MOFs and MOF-based materials. In contrast to many other porous materials, MOFs have both high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacities and outstanding $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ selectivities for capture in the presence of other gasses. This largely stems from MOFs’ unique properties such as structural flexibility, the potential for framework post-synthetic modification, and relatively facile composite formation. Many of these strategies have only recently been developed and further optimization promises to improve MOFs’ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture properties as more work is performed. However, many challenges regarding the preparation and application of MOFs with high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capacities remain unaddressed. For example, many MOFs still have low $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ selectivities in the presence of moisture/water/acidic gasses, even if their overall adsorption capacities are high. Additionally, under these conditions, many MOFs chosen for studies lack the requisite stability for practical application. In the future, it would be necessary to choose MOFs which are known to have high chemical and thermal stability, as found for MOF-808.242,243 In this context, it is important to recall that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture from large stationary sources such as the flue gas of power plants is regarded as one of the most promising avenues to mitigate rising atmospheric $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ concentrations. Generally, flue gas from power plants $\mathrm{\bar{C}O}_{2}$ point sources) is composed of $15\mathrm{-}16\%$ carbon dioxide, $5\mathrm{-}7\%$ water vapor, $73\mathrm{-}77\%$ nitrogen, $3\mathrm{-}4\%$ oxygen and traces of acidic gasses such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{x},$ $\mathrm{so}_{x}$ .109 Consequently, during adsorption, gasses with higher polarities and/or binding energies such as $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ or acidic gasses are preferentially adsorbed over $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ on the OMSs of MOFs. This has to be considered when selecting a MOF for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture in the presence of water. Many pre- and post-synthetic strategies have been developed for improving the water and acid stabilities of those MOFs already under consideration.34,225,243–246 Nevertheless, there are suites of MOFs as yet uninvestigated which may well be more appropriate for this application. Additionally, certain strategies can be accompanied by new issues such as dramatic reduction of the materials’ specific surface areas and/or pore volumes. These issues are beginning to be addressed as we learn how to balance functionalization with maintaining crystallinity and porosity. Finally, for many MOFs, direct capture of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at low partial pressures remains a challenge due to relatively weak $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -framework interactions. Although $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities at low partial pressures can be improved by introducing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\cdot$ philic moieties on MOFs’ organic linkers, adsorbent regeneration energies also typically sharply increase.199,208 This can, of course be overcome by precisely tuning the chemistry between the frameworks and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . In the future, it is likely that high stability and high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity will be targeted in order to position MOFs as the next-generation of materials for practical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture.
## 3. MOFs for catalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion
The modular, tunable, and porous nature of MOFs make them ideal candidates for heterogeneous catalysis. In particular, catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion has been a target. In addition to MOFs, many MOF composites and MOF derivatives have been reported as efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion catalysts. In MOF composites, additional components are hypothesized to serve as new active sites and/or cooperative agents. This chemically emphasizes the respective functional advantages of both MOFs and the added components. MOF composites are typically more stable than the parent MOF(s), leading to improved recyclability and turnover. This area of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion using MOFs is nascent and requires more fundamental studies dealing with reactivity, optimization of performance, study of the long-term properties of the materials, and cyclability. When utilized as templates or precursors, stable MOF derivatives can be obtained. To a large degree, MOF-derived porous materials are able to inherit the salient properties of pristine MOFs, such as high surface area, tailorable porosity, adjustable morphology, and compositional diversity. Additionally, the process of pyrolyzing MOF composites has the potential to create additional active sites, improved conductivity and other important structural and chemical features which are not present in the parent frameworks. Therefore, many MOF-derived porous materials have the potential to affect enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ transformation in comparison to their parent MOF analogues. In this section, we describe $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion reactions performed using MOF-based catalysts, mainly focusing on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ organic transformation into organic products, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation, photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
## 3.1. Heterogeneous $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ organic transformation
A variety of MOF-based materials have been reported for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ organic transformation. The scope of catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversions to organic products has been summarized in Table 2.
3.1.1. Conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ into organic products using MOFs. MOFs have high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities and adjustable pore apertures and cavities. Many of the same active sites applied to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture, including OMSs or LASs, Brønsted acid sites (BASs) and LBSs, can convert $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ into chemical products by organic transformation. This is frequently analogous to the transformations performed by numerous conventional homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts. The utilization of MOFs’ intrinsic functionalities for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ –epoxide cycloaddition reactions has been widely explored. This class of reactions can yield products with a variety of applications. Specifically, cyclic carbonates, the major product of many $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions, have been widely applied as intermediates for the production of engineering plastics, electrolyte solvents for lithium-ion batteries, polar aprotic solvents, degreasers, and fuel additives.301,302
Generally, the mechanism of MOF-based $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ epoxide cycloaddition involves coordination of an epoxide oxygen atom with a LAS and/or a BAS in a MOF-based material (Scheme 2). This coordination polarizes the C–O bond which is then heterolytically cleaved with concurrent nucleophilic attack of the less sterically hindered carbon atom of the epoxide by a co-catalystbased halide anion (a typical co-catalyst is tetrabutylammonium bromide, TBAB) to form a halo-alkoxide intermediate. Subsequently, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ carbon atom, which is frequently activated by LBSs through dipolar interactions, is nucleophilically attacked by the ring-opened alkoxide intermediate and the cyclic carbonate is obtained by intramolecular ring-closure, regenerating the catalyst and co-catalyst. Interestingly, some MOFs can exhibit excellent catalytic activities in the absence of co-catalyst.247–255 Unfortunately, on account of the limited types and relatively low activities of active sites in the MOFs studied for this application, as well as the intrinsic inertness of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , high temperatures and/or pressures must inevitably be applied in these cases. In this context, several strategies for improving the catalytic activities of MOFs towards $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions have been developed. Above all, it is inferred that the natures, quantities and locations of the active sites have enormous impacts on the catalytic efficiencies of the materials.
To support this inference, numerous reports exist. In one case, a porous 3D framework, $[(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{Zn}_{2})_{1.5}(\mathrm{L})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]$ $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{3}\mathbf{L}=10$ -(4- carboxy-phenyl)- $\cdot10H\cdot$ -phenoxazine-3,6-dicarboxylic acid) in which the parent $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ ions could be partially exchanged by $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ ions was reported (Fig. 11a).256 Upon post synthetic exchange, no obvious enhancement was observed in any of the frameworks’ BET surface areas, pore volumes, or $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities. However, the yield of the propylene oxide– $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition product, propylene carbonate, was dramatically reduced from $99\%$ in the zinc MOF to $50\%$ in the cobalt analogue and $32\%$ in the copper MOF (Fig. 11b).
Since the post-synthetic metal exchange process mainly occurred at the dimeric paddlewheel $\mathrm{{(Zn_{2}(C O O)_{4})}}$ SBUs, the tremendous difference between the catalytic activities of these MOFs was mainly attributed to the presence or absence of various metal species. Molecular dynamics (MD) simulations indicated that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions are limited by the energy gap between the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) of the epoxides and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Upon $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding to an OMS, a decrease of LUMO energy is observed, reducing the energy gap and accelerating the reaction. The simulations ultimately demonstrated that the energy gap between the LUMO of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the epoxide’s HOMO increases in the order of $Z{\mathrm{n-Zn~}}<$ $\mathrm{Zn-CO}\mathrm{~<~}\mathrm{Zn-CU}\mathrm{~<~}\mathrm{Co-CO}\mathrm{~<~}\mathrm{Cu-Cu}$ (Fig. 11c), which was in accordance with experimental results and reflected the effect of the nature of the active sites on the catalytic capabilities of MOFs. As further evidence, it was discovered that the catalytic efficiency of a Hf-based MOF (Hf-NU-1000) for the cycloaddition reaction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with styrene oxide (SO) was superior to that of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -based NU-1000 under the same reaction conditions.257 The typical dissociation enthalpy of typical $\mathbf{\Delta}_{\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{O}}$ bonds in NU-1000 is $776\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ . In contrast, the dissociation enthalpy of Hf–O bonds of Hf-NU-1000 is relatively high $(802\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})_{,}$ , leading to the higher oxophilicity of Hf in NU-1000’s SBUs, enhancing the Brønsted acidity and thus the catalytic activity of Hf-NU-1000.
With respect to the quantity and position of active sites, an interesting example is found in the nbo framework MMCF-2. This compound, which is isoreticular to MOF-505, was synthesized from 1,4,7,10-tetrazazcyclododecane- $\cdot N,N^{\prime},N^{\prime\prime},N^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -tetra$p$ -methylbenzoic acid ( $\mathrm{\tilde{H}}_{4}$ tactmb), yielding a framework containing a high density of well-oriented LASs.258 In contrast to MOF-505’s $[1,1^{\prime}$ -biphenyl]- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\big(H_{4}b p t c\big)}$ linker, the azo macrocycles of $\mathbf{H}_{4}$ tactmb acted as copper chelating agents. Therefore, six center-oriented copper sites were added within the cuboctahedral cage of MMCF-2, resulting in a $50\%$ increase of the amount of LASs in MMCF-2. Based on this increase in the quantity of active sites, MMCF-2 displayed a remarkably increased catalytic activity $(95.4\%$ yield) for the cycloaddition of propylene oxide (PO) and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the presence of a co-catalyst under mild reaction conditions (room temperature and 1 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure). This activity surpassed both MOF-505 $\left(48.0\%\right)$ and HKUST-1 $\left(49.2\%\right)$ and was mainly attributed to the higher density of preferentially-oriented active sites (LASs) in the framework that promoted interactions with substrates (PO). To improve MOFs’ reactivities for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions, salen-, porphyrin- and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine-containing organic linkers can also serve as metal chelating agents to diversify and increase the quantities of metal active sites in MOFs.259–261,286 As an example, a multi-component MOF, PCN-900(Eu), fabricated from a combination of $\mathrm{Eu}_{6}$ clusters, tetratopic porphyrinic linkers, and linear linkers can be modified by post-synthetic exchange of the latter and/or metalation of the former linkers to form three isoreticular compounds, PCN-900(Eu)-BPYDC, PCN-900(Eu)-CoTCPP, and PCN-900(Eu)-CoTCPP-CoBPYDC.261 With the highest density of LASs $\left(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\right.$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions), PCN-900(Eu)- CoTCPP-CoBPYDC exhibited the highest catalytic activity for the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}.$ –PO cycloaddition reaction under mild conditions, surpassing that of the non-metalated counterpart (PCN-900(Eu)-BPYDC) or the partially metalated framework (PCN-900(Eu)-CoTCPP).
Taking advantage of synergetic effects between multiple active sites can also boost the catalytic efficiencies of MOF catalysts for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions. Specifically, Lewis acid–Lewis base synergy,109,249,253,255,272,279,280 Lewis acid–Brønsted acid synergy,155,264–268,291 Lewis acid–Lewis acid synergy,256,261,286 and Lewis acid–Brønsted acid–Lewis base synergy270 have been studied for this series of reactions. Employing 2,4-bis(3,5- dicarboxyphenylamino)-6-ol triazine $\left(\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{BDPO}\right)$ as an organic linker has led to the synthesis of a stable $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ -based MOF, JUC-1000, in which the phenol, amino, and triazine groups serve as weak acids and/or bases. This combination of acidic and basic moieties in one material improved the MOF’s chemical stability due to the presence of $-\mathbf{O}^{-}/\mathrm{-}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{H}$ , $-\mathbf{NH-/-NH}^{+}-$ and $-\mathbf{N}{=}\jmath\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}^{+}{=}$ pairs facilitating a so-called ‘‘buffering effect’’.270 The synergy between multiple catalytic sites in JUC-1000, including open $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ metal sites (LASs), phenol hydroxyl groups (BASs), and the LBSs from both the –NH– and triazine groups, allowed the system to produce propylene carbonate in $96\%$ yield under ambient conditions with the aid of a co-catalyst. The authors speculated that the epoxides were synergistically activated by the open $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ Lewis site, and the –OH and –NH– functional groups from the organic linkers by dipolar interactions and/or hydrogen bonding. Additionally, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules were activated upon adsorption, as facilitated by the LBSs located in the pores.
Nevertheless, high-densities of active sites and synergetic behavior between them doesn’t always translate to excellent catalytic performance in MOFs. Interactions between the substrates and active sites as well as the facile transport of reactants and products also play crucial roles in epoxide conversion reactions. A combination of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl)ethylene $\left({\bf H}_{4}{\bf T C P E}\right)$ , and L-proline (L-Pro) was used to form two new (4,4)-network, Ni(II)-based MOFs, Ni-TCPE1 and Ni-TCPE2, that were hypothesized to catalyze $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ –epoxide cycloaddition.271 In contrast to the 1D quadrilateral channels $(17.9\times17.9\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2})$ ) present in Ni-TCPE2, Ni-TCPE1 possesses large single-walled nanotubes (exterior wall diameter: $3.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ ; interior channel diameter: $2.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), liberating active sites within the framework and facilitating the transfer of both substrates and products. In this context, although both MOFs possessed an identical amount of OMSs, a total turnover number (TON) of 17 500 per mole of catalyst after 10 repetitions of the reaction $\left(32.5\mathrm{h}\right)$ was obtained when using Ni-TCPE1, whereas it took over $^{70\mathrm{h}}$ for Ni-TCPE2 to achieve the same value. As an unfortunate drawback, the utilization of a homogeneous halide co-catalyst cannot be avoided in most of the approaches detailed above. This serves to complicate the implementation, and increase the costs of these catalytic systems.254 As an alternative approach, such co-catalysts can be immobilized onto MOFs’ backbones using pre- or post-synthetic modification, opening new pathways toward the realization of truly heterogeneous catalytic systems. As a very early example of such tethering, a co-catalyst-functionalized MOF (F-IRMOF-3) was made by nucleophilic substitution of the amine groups of IRMOF-3 using methyl iodide.249 The yield of propylene carbonate (PC) produced by F-IRMOF-3 quickly increased to $98\%$ in $1.5\mathrm{h}$ from $2\%$ in unfunctionalized IRMOF-3 $(5\ \mathrm{h})$ . Also, a similar process was used to synthesize a bifunctional imidazolium decorated UiO-66 variant, $\left(\mathrm{I}^{-}\right)$ Meim-UiO-66, (Fig. 12).254 Taking advantage of the coexistence of BASs $\left(\mathbf{Zr}\mathbf{-OH}/\right.$ $\mathbf{Zr{\bar{{OH}}}_{2,}}$ ) and LBSs $(\mathrm{I}^{-})$ , $\left(\mathrm{I}^{-}\right)$ Meim-UiO-66 exhibited fantastic catalytic activity for the cycloaddition of epichlorohydrin (ECH) and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ under relatively mild conditions ( $\mathrm{120^{\circ}C}$ and 1 atm $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\cdot$ ), with a yield of $93\%$ . Furthermore, due to the heterogeneous nature of the catalyst, no significant loss of catalytic activity was observed during six sequential reaction trials. Most significantly, each subsequent trial could be conducted without the addition of any halide co-catalyst. Therefore, one can conclude that in order to achieve efficient catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition under mild conditions, it is desirable to construct MOFs possessing a large number of accessible active sites, large pores, synergistic effects between multiple active sites, and organic linker-tethered halide ions.
As analogues to epoxides, aziridines, a class of three-membered heterocyclic compounds with one amine group, can be converted to oxazolidinones, a versatile class of compounds commonly applied as antimicrobial agents. Such a reaction was reported in a 3D $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ -MOF (ZnGlu) synthesized from glutamate and zinc sulfate heptahydrate.272 Utilizing LASs $(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+})$ , LBSs $\left(-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)$ , and a TBAB co-catalyst, about $90\%$ of 2-methylaziridine was converted to 4-methyloxazolidin-2-one at room temperature under $1\ \mathrm{MPa}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , with a high selectivity of $99\%$ . As an additional example, tetrakis-3,5-bis[(4-carboxy)phenyl]phenyl porphine $\left(\mathbf{H_{10}T B C P P P}\right)$ was used as an organic linker to synthesize a copper porphyrinbased MOF.294 At room temperature, under 1 bar of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure, the MOF converted 1-methyl-2-phenylaziridine to 3-methyl-5- phenyl-2-oxazolidinone with a moderate yield of $63\%$ , higher than that of HKUST-1 $(47\%)$ under similar reaction conditions. The amplified catalytic performance of the copper porphyrin based MOF in this study was attributed to the increased accessibility of Lewis-acidic $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ sites.
An alternative $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion route is found in the oxidative carboxylation of olefins following a two-step process involving the epoxidation of olefins and subsequent epoxide– $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition. The development of such tandem reactivity in one material is highly desirable and economical due to the availability and potential complexity of the olefin substrates. Unfortunately, oxidative carboxylation of olefins generally requires high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressures and yields numerous byproducts with low carbonate yields.292 A zirconium-based MOF, MOF-892, with dual acidic sites (LASs from $\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{(w)}$ and BASs from uncoordinated carboxylates) was utilized as a catalyst for the one-pot synthesis of styrene carbonate (SC) from styrene and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .291 Remarkably, in the presence of tertbutyl hydroperoxide (TBHP) and TBAB, styrene was completely converted in MOF-892 at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in only $^{\mathrm{~9~h~}}$ . Furthermore, the reaction was $80\%$ selective for SC with $t{\cdot}$ -butanol and benzaldehyde as the major byproducts. Targeting synergistic effects between multiple components, Keggin-type $\mathrm{[ZnW_{12}O_{40}]^{6-}}$ anions (oxidant), zinc(II) ions (LASs), $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -BPY (LBSs) and pyrrolidine-2-yl-imidazole (PYI, asymmetric organocatalytic groups) were incorporated within a single MOF (Fig. 13).293 The resultant polyoxometalate-based MOFs exhibited not only excellent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition catalytic efficiency $(>99\%$ yield) and high enantioselectivity $(90-96\%$ ee) with $(R)$ or (S)-styrene oxide (SO) but could also convert SO into phenyl(ethylene carbonate) in $\sim4$ days with high yield $(90-92\%)$ and ee $(77-80\%)$ at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 0.5 MPa $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .
Carboxylation of terminal alkynes, yielding alkynyl carboxylic acids or esters, is also a sought-after $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion reaction to which MOF-based materials have been applied. These products are highly significant to pharmaceutical and fine chemical applications.303,304 Indeed, many catalytic systems have been developed for these reactions.305 However, most of these systems feature nonporous materials, dramatically hindering mass transport and active site accessibility. Under the assumption that they can more competently affect these terminal alkyne carboxylations, MOFs have been investigated for such reactions. For instance, inspired by the excellent terminal alkyne carboxylation catalytic performance of CuI and the facile conversion of CuI to $[\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{y}]$ under solvothermal conditions, a pair of two cluster-based MOFs assembled from multinuclear Gd-clusters and Cu-clusters $\left(\mathrm{[{Cu}_{12}\mathbf{I}_{12}]}\right)$ or $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathbf{I}_{2}\right])$ were made.297 Both MOFs afforded encouraging yields of $n$ -butyl 2-alkynoate $(74-80\%)$ as the product of phenylacetylene carboxylation at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure in the presence of $\mathbf{Cs}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ and $n$ -BuI.
More recently, based on the specific alkynophilicity of silver complexes, a porous silver coordination polymer was synthesized, in which the decentralized silver(I) chains were shown to activate the $\mathbf{C}{\equiv}\mathbf{C}$ bonds of propargylic alcohols and subsequently cyclize them with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .298 In the presence of this $\mathrm{\bfAg(\mathrm{_I)}}$ -based MOF and triphenylphosphine $\left(\mathrm{Ph}_{3}\mathrm{P}\right)$ , propargylic alcohols were converted to the target products ((Z)-5-benzylidene-4,4-dimethyl1,3-dioxolan-2-one) with excellent yields $(>89\%)$ under $0.5\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ within $36\mathrm{h}$ . Another exciting example is found in the application of an organosulfonate-based MOF, TMOF-3, with a defective pcu topology.299 In this MOF, the missing linker defects were ordered, providing an array of linker-pendant sulfonate groups for post-synthetic $\mathrm{\sfAg(I)}$ metalation (Fig. 14). In the post-synthetically modified material, the sulfonate groups on the organic linkers had a high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ affinity and the Ag(I) ions could activate alkynes. Therefore, the synergy in the resultant framework allowed it to catalyze the carboxylic cyclization of propargylic alcohols in the presence of 1,8-diazabicyclo[5.4.0]underc-7-ene (DBU) under 0.1 MPa $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure at room temperature, with satisfying yields $(>86\%)$ for most substrates. Encouraged by the high catalytic performance of the $\mathrm{\sfAg(I)}$ ions towards propargylic alcohol cyclization, the $\mathrm{\bfAg(\mathrm{_I)}}$ -decorated sulfonate-MOF was also employed to catalyze other two $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion reaction, including three-component reactions between propargyl alcohols, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and primary amines, as well as carboxylic cyclization of propargyl amines with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , yielding oxazolidinones which are significant components of antibiotics. After 15 hours, the Ag(I)-decorated sulfonate-MOF converted propargyl alcohols into oxazolidinone derivatives with high yields $(>97\%)$ in the present of $\mathrm{Ph}_{3}\mathrm{P}$ under mild reaction conditions. Additionally, upon DBU addition, carboxylic propargyl amine cyclization was achieved under extremely mild conditions (1 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and room temperature) after $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ , with high yield $(>89\%)$ . Notably, all these catalytic results were determined by $^1\mathrm{H\NMR}$ , as an average value of three runs, indicating their reliability and repeatability.
As previously mentioned, copper based MOFs also show great potential for alkyne activation.297 By employing 5-aminonicotinic acid (L5) as the organic linker, a unique, three dimensional, anionic framework, $\begin{array}{r l r}{\lefteqn{\big\{\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{6}\big)_{0.75}\big[\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{4}\mathbf{I}_{4}\cdot\big(\mathbf{L}\boldsymbol{5}\big)_{3}-}\big]}\end{array}$ $\left(\mathrm{In}\right)_{0.75}\mathbf{\]}{\cdot}\mathbf{DMF}{\cdot}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right\}$ , was constructed and applied to the catalysis of carboxylic cyclization of various propargylic alcohols.300 With the addition of trace triethylamine (TEA), various propargylic alchohols were smoothly converted to the corresponding products in this MOF without addition of solvent based on the synergistic effect between $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ and $\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{In})$ in this heterometallic framework. Not limited to these metal centers, the Lewis basic sites on the organic linkers can also affect chemical transformation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . As a example, the reaction of an amino tripodal imidazole ligand ( $\mathrm{\Delta}N^{1}$ -(4-( $1H\cdot$ -imidazol-1-yl)benzyl)- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf N^{1}}$ -(2-aminoethyl) ethane1,2-diamine) with $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ ions gave a flexible MOF decorated with $\left.-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right.$ groups, which exhibited decent catalytic performance for the carboxylative cyclization of propargylamines with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with desirable TON (9300).323 The authors speculated that the amidogen ligand played an important role in facilitating ring closure to yield the oxazolidinone derivatives, which was fairly stable under the reaction conditions.
3.1.2. Conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to organic products using MOF composites. MOFs impregnated with metal oxides, noble metal NPs, and other catalytically active species, also known as MOF composites, have been studied as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion catalysts. As a representative example, $\sim1.4\ \mathrm{nm\Ag\NPs}$ were incorporated into the cavities of MIL-101 using a liquid impregnation-reduction method to obtain a series of $\mathbf{Ag@MIL-101}$ composites.306 The resultant materials had excellent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities and were decorated with unsaturated $\mathrm{{Cr}(m)}$ sites and highly active Ag NPs that were stabilized by MIL-101. The resultant $\mathbf{Ag(a)MIL-101}$ $\left(4.16~\mathrm{wt^{9}/_{0}~A g}\right)$ exhibited remarkable catalytic activity ( $96.5\%$ yield) and excellent stability over 5 runs of terminal alkyne carboxylation reactions utilizing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ under extremely mild conditions (1 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , $50\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ ). By employing similar processes, Ag NPs were encapsulated in the pores of MIL-100(Fe) and UiO-66 to give $\mathbf{Ag}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})\mathbf{MIL}{-}100(\mathbf{Fe})$ and Ag@UIO-66.307 In the presence of $\mathbf{Cs}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ (base) and DMF (solvent), each MOF composite carboxylated terminal alkynes in good yield $(>94\%)$ at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
MOFs’ surfaces can also serve as supports for pre-synthesized metal nanoparticles (MNPs).69 As examples of this application, monometallic $\mathbf{Ag}\mathbf{NPs}$ and bimetallic Pd–Cu NPs were separately loaded on the surface of two different MOFs (Co-MOF and MIL-101) for carbon dioxide fixation via terminal alkyne carboxylation.308,309 Using $\mathbf{Cs}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ as a base, both resultant MOF composites $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{Ag}/\mathbf{Co-MOF}\right.$ and $\mathbf{Pd\mathrm{-}C u/M I L{-}}101\$ ) displayed high catalytic activities (yield ${>}96\%$ ) at room temperature. More recently, an electrostatic attraction strategy was applied to incorporate atomically precise nanoclusters (APNCs) into MOFs.310 The resultant composite, $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{Au_{12}A g_{32}(S R)_{30}@Z I F-8}}$ , not only adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ but also activated terminal alkynes. Therefore, outstanding catalytic performance in the synthesis of phenylpropiolic acid from $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and phenylacetylene under mild conditions (1 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure, $50~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ with a high TON of 18 164 and good recyclability was observed.
MOF composites also play unique roles in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ –epoxide cycloaddition reactions.311–315 Recently, a new strategy to imprison chiral salen $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{III}))$ complexes within the pores of IRMOF-3 was developed.311 The $(R,R){\cdot}\mathrm{salen}(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{m}))$ complexes were adsorbed in the pores of IRMOF-3, followed by decoration of the organic linkers with acetic anhydride by PSM. The chiral composite $\scriptstyle{\left(R,R\right)}$ -Salen(Co)@IRMOF-3-AM) gave an acceptable PO conversion of $25\%$ and a PC ee of $21\%$ . Following this, two Salen-Cu(II) complexes (Salen-tBu-Cu(II) and Salen- $\operatorname{\cdotH}_{2}\operatorname{-Cu}(\operatorname{II}))$ were encapsulated inside the pores of MIL-101 using a ‘‘ship in a bottle’’ approach to obtain bifunctional catalysts for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ epoxide cycloaddition.312 Salen-tBu-Cu(II)@MIL-101 with $0.3\ \mathrm{g}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ Salen-tBu-Cu(II) loading gave the highest catalytic conversion $\left(87.8\%\right)$ of PO in the presence of TBAB under extremely mild conditions $(25\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C},$ 1 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure).
As with MOFs, a goal of heterogeneous catalysis in MOF composites is to avoid the use of a co-catalyst. Toward this end, imidazolium-based poly(ionic liquid)s (polyILs) were confined in the cavities of MIL-101 using an in situ polymerization strategy (Fig. 15).313 In the absence of co-catalyst, $94\%$ of ECH was converted into the targeted product with high selectivity $(>99\%)$ in polyILs $\textcircled{2}$ MIL-101 at $70^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ and 1 bar of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ after $24\mathrm{h}$ , with no significant decrease in catalytic performance after ten reactions. Similarly, a linear ionic polymer was inserted into the pores of MIL-101 to give a composite (MIL-101-IP).314 With respect to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}.$ –ECH cycloaddition, the authors found that MIL-101-IP ( $99\%$ yield) outperformed the ionic polymer ( $3\%$ yield), MIL-101 ( $32\%$ yield), and the physical mixture of the two individual components ( $80\%$ yield) under the same conditions. Kinetic studies indicated that the reaction’s rate constant doubled in MIL-101-IP compared to that of the physical mixture at room temperature. Such work demonstrates a depleted energy requirement to obtain the similar conversion results in MIL-101-IP.
New components can also endow the corresponding composites with emergent functions/properties.315 For example, templated growth and anchoring of ZIF-8 crystals on carbon nitrides (CNs), which possess micrometer-sized pores and high nitrogen contents, gave ZIF-8/CN foams with hierarchical porosities.315 The composites were highly hydrophobic as evidenced by their water contact angles in excess of $135^{\circ}$ . The $24\mathrm{~h~}$ conversion of ECH under $1\mathrm{MPa}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was greatly improved in the ZIF-8/ CN foam ( $100\%$ with $100\%$ selectivity to chloropropene carbonate) compared to that of the pristine ZIF-8 (conversion: $84.1\%$ ; selectivity: $52\%$ ). The significantly increased catalytic performance was mainly ascribed to the hydrophobicity and hierarchical porosity of the ZIF-8/CN foam which effectively improved the transport of reactants to the catalytically active sites.
3.1.3. Conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ into organic products using MOF derivatives. Pyrolysis of MOFs containing transition metals and organic linkers especially those with O, N, and S produces a class of very interesting materials known as MOF derivatives. In such derivatives, a diverse array of active sites can be observed that are not typically found in MOFs.88
As an example of the use of MOF derivatives for these reactions, several zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs; ZIF-7, -8, -9, and -67) were used as precursors to develop a series of N-doped, metal-containing nanoporous carbons, which were applied as catalysts for cyclic carbonate synthesis from epoxides and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .317 Among the obtained carbons, a bifunctional acid–base porous carbon synthesized by pyrolysis of ZIF-9 at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under an Ar stream exhibited the best catalytic activity $90\%$ yield) under $0.6\mathrm{\bf\MPa}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The authors attributed the high activity to uniformly distributed, partly oxidized cobalt NPs and nitrogenous species, which acted as acidic and basic sites, respectively. Recently, employing ZIF-8 as a template and sodium hypochlorite (NaClO) as an oxidant, an oxidized porous carbon was synthesized.318 Upon moderate oxidation, several oxygen-containing functional groups, including carboxyl, lactone and alkoxy, were introduced onto the surface of the N-doped porous carbon without any loss of the original active sites including $z\mathrm{no}$ and pyridinic nitrogen atoms. On the basis of the porosity inherited from ZIF-8, the synergistic effects between the multiple catalytic sites and the aid of TBAB, the optimized catalyst, $\mathrm{ZnO@NPC-Ox-700}$ (NPC: N-doped porous carbon), exhibited high catalytic conversion, selectivity and recyclability for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition with diverse epoxides under mild conditions $(25\mathrm{-}60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 1 atm of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ).
These two reports demonstrate the potential for high activity and stability in MOF derivative-catalyzed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion. It is likely that the approach can be extended to other MOFs, opening new avenues to catalysts with enhanced activities, stabilities, and selectivities for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ organic transformation.
## 3.2. Heterogeneous hydrogenation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$
$\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation produces feedstock chemicals such as methane, methanol, alkyl formates and formic acid. Such reactions have been extensively studied in recent years.324,325 Homogeneous catalysts frequently show satisfactory activity but need to be utilized under very high total pressures (20–200 bar)
and are difficult to separate from catalytic systems.325 As an alternative, heterogeneous catalysts such as the industriallyapplied ternary $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathbf{ZnO}/\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ are desirable in terms of catalyst separability, stability, and applicability. Unfortunately, reactions utilizing such systems always employ high temperatures $\left(200-300\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ and pressures (50–100 bar) as heterogeneous catalytic reactions have higher activation barriers than their homogeneous counterparts.326 In contrast, regarding heterogeneous $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation, MOFs possess the following advantages: (1) structural designability; MOFs can incorporate highly reactive frustrated Lewis pairs (LASs and LBSs) in a single material.327–329 (2) porosity and availability of organic coordinating groups; classical catalysts such as metals, metal oxides, metal complexes, and metal clusters can be confined and/or stabilized in MOFs, and can cooperate with MOFs to augment their respective strengths and offset their respective drawbacks.330–340 (3) certain MOFs can be thermally decomposed; a thermally converted MOF yields metal/metal oxide nanoparticles in a porous carbon matrix with uniform dispersion of active sites and enhanced thermal and chemical stability, resulting in highly efficient conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ under a variety of reaction conditions.341–348 As summarized in Table 3, some MOF-based materials have been reported to catalyze $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation reactions under various conditions.
As an example of heterogeneous $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation achieved by a MOF, DFT calculations were used to study the catalytic behavior of UiO-66 functionalized with frustrated Lewis pairs (FLPs).327 The calculated reaction mechanism involved first heterolytic dissociation of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ by the FLPs, then, in the second step a hydride and a proton were added to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in a concerted fashion, producing formic acid (HCOOH).327,328 Through the studies, it was determined that $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ heterolysis was the critical step in the catalytic reaction. The drawback in such a mechanism, however, was that the MOF must preferentially bind $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ over $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . To this end, the same researchers developed a new catalyst for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction based on the expanded, isoreticular framework, UiO-67.329 In UiO-67, more FLPs could be introduced without being quenched by adjacent FLP moieties. Furthermore, in the material, $\mathbf{UiO-}67\big(\mathbf{NBF}_{2}\big)_{4}.$ heterolytic dissociation of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ was found to be much easier than $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption due to the stronger binding of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ over $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with adsorption energies of $-0.22$ and $-0.50\ \mathrm{~eV}$ for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , respectively. This dramatically reduced each activation energy for carbon dioxide methanolization. These calculated results suggested that MOFs could be adept $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation catalysts. Recently, molecular iridium catalysts were incorporated into two UiO-type MOFs by anchoring $\mathrm{Ir}^{3+}$ to the $\mathbf{N}$ atoms of bpydc linkers.330 The resultant catalysts were placed in the condensing chamber of a Soxhlet extractor for hydrogenation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to formate. This special reaction device maximized contact between the two reaction gases ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ${\bf H}_{2}^{\mathrm{~\tiny~.~}}$ ) and solvent $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)$ , and neutralized the solution using sodium bicarbonate $\left(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{HCO}_{3}\right)$ efficiently shifting the reaction $\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{g})+\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\mathrm{g})\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{HCO}_{2}\mathrm{H}(\mathrm{aq})$ toward formic acid formation. Moreover, the ligand’s hydroxyl group acted as an electron-donating group and a hydrogen-bonding site, further facilitating the conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , resulting in a high TOF of $410\pm3\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ under 1 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{2}:\mathbf{CO}_{2}=1:1)$ and $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .
Various metal catalysts can be combined with MOFs, yielding hybrid materials designed to catalyze $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation reactions. As an example of such material, Cu nanocrystals $(\sim18~\mathrm{nm})$ were encapsulated within UiO-66 to yield a composite catalyst $(\mathrm{Cu}{}=\mathrm{UiO}{}\cdot\ 66)$ for highly selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol (Fig. 16a).331 The catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Cu}{\subset}\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66$ was investigated under a 10 bar gas mixture $\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}:\mathbf{H}_{2}=1:3\right)$ at $175^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The initial turnover frequency (TOF) of MeOH formation was $3.7~\times~10^{-3}~\mathrm{{s^{-1}}}$ , more than 8 times greater than that of the conventional $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathbf{ZnO}/\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ catalyst. More importantly, no byproducts were detected at any reaction temperature in the range of $175\mathrm{-}250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) suggested that the $\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{\left(w\right)}$ atoms in UiO-66 were reduced upon coming into contact with the Cu nanocrystals. This indicated that the improved TOF and selectivity of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Cu}\subset\mathbf{UiO-}66$ over $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathbf{ZnO}/\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ were caused by the increased contact area between the SBUs and the Cu nanocrystals. Different sized Ni NPs incorporated in MOFs also resulted in varied catalysts for CO2 hydrogenation to methane.332–334
Integrating MOFs with two or more metal components may lead to unexpected $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation catalytic activity.335,336 In order to hydrogenate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to MeOH, both Pt nanocubes $(\sim6\ \mathrm{nm})$ and Au nanocages $\left(\sim31\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ were embedded within ZIF-8 to form a composite, Au&Pt $\textcircled{2}Z\mathbf{I}\mathbf{F}$ .335 Under irradiation by a Xe lamp, the heat generated by the photothermal effects of Au nanocages was insulated by ZIF-8. This improved the catalytic activity of the Pt nanocubes. The resultant catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation reaction had a high TOF number of $1522\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . In fact, even at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the Au&Pt@ZIF $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation catalyst was still active.
Although the noble MNPs in the abovementioned materials exhibit desirable $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation catalytic efficiencies, nonnoble metal oxide catalysts are more economical and thus may be a better option for such conversions. Toward this end, a series of ${\sf a}{\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}}{\bf O}_{3}/{\sf Z I F{-}}8$ catalysts were assembled in which $\sim30\ \mathrm{nm}$ ${\tt a}{\cdot}{\mathrm{F}}{\mathrm{e}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ NPs were dispersed on the ZIF-8 supports.337 Under a 3 MPa $\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}:\mathbf{H}_{2}=1:3\right)$ pressure at $573\mathrm{~K~}$ , ${\sf a}{\sf-}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}/{\sf Z I F}{\cdot}8$ supported on $150\ \mathrm{nm}$ ZIF-8 crystals displayed considerable $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion and light olefin (C1–C4) selectivity. More recently, a novel composite catalyst, $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathbf{ZnO}_{x}\mathbf{\textcircled{2}U i O}$ -bpy with $<1\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{{Cu}}/\mathrm{{ZnO}}_{x}$ NPs, was made by two post-synthetic metalations of UiO-bpy with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ , respectively, and subsequent in situ reduction at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using a binary $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ mixture (total pressure: $4\ \mathrm{MPa}$ ; $\mathbf{H}_{2}:\mathbf{CO}_{2}=3:1\mathbf{\check{\Gamma}}$ ) (Fig. 16b).338 In comparison with the industrial ternary $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathbf{ZnO}/\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ catalyst, the strong metal–support interactions (SMSIs) and confinement effects from UiO-bpy effectively prevented Cu NP aggregation. The phase separation between Cu and $\mathbf{ZnO}_{x}$ preserved the number of active interfaces. At $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and under a constant flow of $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}_{2}\left(3/1\right)$ at $4\ \mathrm{{MPa}}$ , $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}\mathbf{\textcircled{\mathrm{{a}M O F}}}$ presented a high spacetime yield of $2.59\mathrm{g_{MeOH}}\mathrm{kg_{Cu}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with $100\%$ MeOH selectivity, as well as high stability over $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ . However, for the $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathbf{ZnO}/\$ $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ catalyst, only $0.83\mathrm{g_{MeOH}}\mathrm{kg_{Cu}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ was obtained with low MeOH selectivity of $54.8\%$ under the same conditions, possibly caused by competing catalytic reactions such as reverse water gas shift (RWGS).
In addition to metal/metal oxide NPs, other highly active metal species can also be incorporated within MOFs in order to hydrogenate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . For example, dinuclear $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}$ clusters were homogenously incorporated into a bimetallic MOF $\mathrm{\{Ca^{II}C u_{6}^{I I}}}$ [(S,S)-metho $\mathbf{x}]_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\}{\cdot}16\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ .339 Under a gas mixture $\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}:\mathbf{N}_{2}:\mathbf{H}_{2}=1:1:4\right)$ ) at 4 bar and $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the composite had a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ methanation TOF of $3.1~\times~10^{-4}~\mathrm{{s^{-1}}}$ , outperforming several industrial catalysts such as ${\mathrm{Ru}}{-}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ . Recently, a facile method to immobilize a homogenous $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation catalyst, $(^{t_{\mathrm{Bu}}}\mathrm{PNP})\mathrm{Ru(CO)HCl}$ , in the pores of UiO-66 was developed.340 The process was based on the relative size of the catalyst with respect to the MOF’s pore metrics as the catalyst is larger than the pore apertures of UiO-66 but smaller than the pore size (Fig. 17).
In the hybrid material, catalytic hydrogenation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to formate was investigated in a DMF solution under 3 bar $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and 12 bar $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $300\mathrm{K}$ . The TON of the hybrid catalyst was comparable to that of the molecular catalyst and the catalytic activity was retained after 5 subsequent reactions. The authors attributed the prolonged activity of the catalyst to impediment of bimolecular catalyst deactivation as is common in heterogeneous catalysis.
Although many MOFs display excellent catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation performance, some are unstable at high temperature, preventing practical application. To remedy this drawback, MOFderived materials were studied and applied.341–348 A highly-active $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ methanation catalyst was generated by in situ pyrolysis of ruthenium (Ru)-impregnated UiO-66 during the catalytic methanation reaction.341 The resultant catalysts were composed of a mixture of $\mathrm{{Ru}^{0}\ N P s}$ $\left(2\mathrm{-}5\ \mathrm{\nm}\right)$ supported by $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ NPs $\left(10{-}20\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ with two crystal forms (tetragonal and monoclinic). In the composites, the $\mathrm{\Ru}^{0}$ NPs served as active sites and the oxygen vacancies of the $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ NPs served as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption sites. The composites with different Ru loadings $\left(0.75{-}2\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ exhibited high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion efficiencies $(>90\%)$ with exceptional methane selectivities (nearly $100\%$ ) at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and under a 5 bar gas mixture $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}:\mathbf{CO}_{2}=4:1\right)$ . More importantly, the remarkable catalytic performance of $1\mathrm{Ru},$ /UiO-66-derived catalyst $\mathrm{\Delta}^{\cdot}1\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Ru loading) was maintained in excess of $160\mathrm{~h~}$ , demonstrating the catalyst’s high stability.
It should be noted that reports of in situ catalyst preparation are very rare. Instead, most MOF-derived materials are formed by calcination at high temperatures, and only after this calcination can each catalyst’s performance be evaluated.342–346 For example, in a two-step process, presynthesized $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ was pyrolyzed in air and the products were then reduced by $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ to afford a PdZn alloy catalyst supported on ZnO.342 The catalyst was applied to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation reactions targeting methanol as the product. After the two-step process, nearly $50\%$ of the surface Pd atoms in the Pd NPs were substituted by Zn atoms, forming $3.7\mathrm{-}5.8\ \mathrm{\nm}$ PdZn alloy NPs. The highest methanol yield over the PdZn alloy catalysts was $0.65\:\mathrm{g}\:\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ at $270^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and a total pressure of $4.5\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}:\mathbf{CO}_{2}=3:1\right)$ . The excellent catalytic activity was attributed to the small size of the PdZn alloy NPs and abundant oxygen defects on the surface of the ZnO. Also, $\mathrm{{Cu/Zn}}$ nanoparticles in a carbon matrix can be prepared as exemplified by the calcination of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -doped HKUST-1 at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under an argon atmosphere.343 In this case, the metals did not sinter, nor did the bimetallic catalyst phase separate, leaving pyrolyzed $15{-}25\ \mathrm{nm\Cu/Zn}$ NPs. The authors attributed this to protection of the metals by the carbon matrix. The $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{\overline{{a}}C}$ catalysts were subsequently applied to the RWGS reaction. A submillimeter-sized $\mathbf{Cu}/\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{\overline{{a}}C}$ sample exhibited the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion $\left(5.0\%\right)$ and CO selectivity $(100\%)$ at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 bar $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}:\mathbf{CO}_{2}=3:1\right)$ . Additionally, the catalyst remained active for over 20 hours. When using MIL-101(Fe) as both the template and self-sacrificing precursor, a core–shell $\mathbf{Fe@C}$ photocatalyst was formed by a two-step calcination approach. The material was then applied to solar-powered $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion using $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ (Fig. 18).344 The pyrolysis process effectively prevented the generation of large Fe NPs at higher temperatures, resulting in the formation of ultrafine Fe NPs $(\sim9.7\ \mathrm{nm})$ . Upon irradiation with UV-light, the Fe NP excitation was amplified by the plasmon–photon coupling effect on the surface of the $\mathbf{Fe@C}$ . This facilitated the formation of energetic hot electrons that activated $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Accordingly, $\mathbf{Fe}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}}\mathbf{C}$ produced $2196.17~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ of CO after light irradiation for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , outperforming both $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ $\left(1963.3~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\right)$ ) and $\mathrm{Fe/CNT}$ $\left(1453.2\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\right)$ under the same reaction conditions. More importantly, the ultrathin carbon layers (1–3 layers) on the Fe NPs significantly facilitated the desorption of CO from the catalyst’s surface of catalyst, leading to high CO selectivity $\left(>99.9\%\right)$ .
Though MOF-derived materials’ sizes clearly play important roles in their catalytic activities, it was also determined that this activity could be regulated by tailoring a MOF’s morphology.345 By utilizing an ionic surfactant, cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB), the morphology of ZIF-67 crystals was transitioned from cubic to rhombic dodecahedron while the particle size increased from $150\mathrm{nm}$ to $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Upon pyrolysis in a $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, Co-based porous carbon catalysts with Co NPs ( $7-20\mathrm{nm}$ in size)
were obtained while the original morphology of the MOF was preserved. Additionally, these Co NPs were well separated by the graphite-like carbon of the resultant catalyst, which effectively prevented metal sintering. When $0.01~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of CTAB was added, the resultant Co-based porous carbon catalyst exhibited a high BET surface area $\left(346\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\right)$ , $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion efficiency $\left(52.5\%\right)$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity $\left(>99.2\%\right)$ under $3\ \mathrm{MPa}$ of gas $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}:\mathbf{CO}_{2}=4:1\right)$ at $270^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ . Additionally, the catalytic performance of MOF derivatives for the hydrogenation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ can be promoted by loading a MOF (Basolite F300) with metals $\left(0.75~\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ using a well-established catalyst preparation method, namely incipient wetness impregnation.346 Specifically, upon potassium loading, the catalyst exhibited improved olefin selectivity with $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{-C_{4}}$ olefin space time yields of 33.6 mmol ${\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{cat}}}^{-1}{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ , on account of the increased $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and CO affinity, while decreasing $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake and the balance between different active phases in the resultant K-doped catalysts.
As an alternative strategy, MOF derivatives for catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion can be synthesized without thermal treatment.
Recently, a water-soaking process created a ZIF-67-derived (ZIF-67 is unstable in water) nanoreactor taking advantage of the phase transitions of $\mathrm{ZIF{-}67@P t@m S i O_{2}}$ $\mathrm{(mSiO_{2}\ =\ }$ mesoporous silica) nanocubes.347 During this phase transition, ZIF-67 acted as both a morphological template and a sacrificial cobalt source to react with the silicic acid from $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ hydrolysis, yielding a cobalt silicate. The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ methanation was catalyzed by the final catalyst, $\mathrm{Pt@CSN}$ (cobalt silicate nanocubes). The two-step reaction mechanism involves (i) CO intermediate formation as the result of RWGS reaction on Pt sites, and (ii) CO methanation over Co sites. The shell structure of the $\mathrm{Pt@CSN}$ nanoreactor not only immobilized the Pt NPs but also offered additional diffusion pathways for reaction gasses and intermediates, prolonging their retention times and eventually boosting the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion and the selectivity toward $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (41.8 and $94\%$ , respectively at 30 bar $\left[\mathbf{CO}_{2}:\mathbf{H}_{2}:\mathbf{N}_{2}=6:18:1\right]$ and $320~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ .
## 3.3. Photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction
One envisions a carbon-neutral energy cycle where any produced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ can be converted to useful fuels and/or commodity chemicals with minimal energy input. To accomplish this, the importance of the application of solar energy to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion is paramount. Although semiconductor materials represent the state-of-the-art with respect to photocatalysis, they are plagued by rapid hole–electron recombination and poor $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, restricting their photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction efficiencies. On the other hand, MOF-based materials have been identified as a new class of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction photocatalysts.45,63,86,90 Their $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture performances are summarized in Table 1. The photocatalytic performances for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction in MOF-based materials are summarized in Table 4.
3.3.1. MOFs for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The unique properties of MOFs position them as ideal platforms for the integration of photosensitizers, catalytic centers and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption sites in a single material to target photocatalytic CO2 reduction.52,63,86,90
As an example of photosensitizer and photocatalyst incorporation in a MOF, BPDC and $[\mathrm{Re^{I}(C O)}_{3}(\mathrm{bpydc)Cl}]$ were used as the organic linkers in the synthesis of a UiO-67 variant.46 In the resultant framework, $[\mathrm{Re^{I}(C O)}_{3}(\mathrm{bpydc)Cl}]$ served as both the photoactive and catalytically active unit, leading to high catalytic activity. With TEA as a sacrificial reducing agent, the resulting mixed-linker UiO-67 exhibited a total CO-TON of 10.9 after a $20\mathrm{h}$ reaction, three times higher than that of the homogeneous $[\mathrm{Re^{I}(C O)}_{3}(\mathrm{bpydc)Cl}]$ catalyst. Such work demonstrates the utility of MOFs’ porous natures. Photocatalysts do not have to be incorporated using a mixed-linker approach, however. In fact, $\mathrm{Ir(ppy)}_{2}(\mathrm{Hdcbpy)}$ was used as the organic linker to directly incorporate a photoactive catalyst into a MOF (Ir-CP) to achieve visible-light mediated $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.349 The Ir-CP exhibited remarkable catalytic performance in the conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to formate using triethanolamine (TEOA) as a sacrificial electron donating agent. Transition metal porphyrins can also be similarly useful in MOFs.350,351 A metalloporphyrinic MOF (ZrPP-1-Co) was applied to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{to-CO}$ photoreduction.351 The uniformly distributed porphyrinic ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions not only adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , enhancing the MOF’s uptake capacity $\left(\sim4.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ at 1 atm and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ ), but also functioned as mediators, promoting electron transfer from linker-based benzene rings to the porphyrin cores to afford ${\mathrm{Co}}^{0}$ in the presence of TEOA. The ${\bf C o}^{0}$ species then activated and converted $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to CO and hydrogenated CO to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with the aid of protonated TEOA.
The photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction activities of MOFs can also be improved by combining photosensitizers and catalytically active sites within a single framework.352–357 A titanium-based MOF, ${\bf N H}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125$ , was applied to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and subsequent photocatalytic reduction to formate under visible-light irradiation.352 In this compound, the light was absorbed by the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC linkers, generating photoelectrons that were then transferred to the Ti centers in the SBUs by a ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) process. The metals then acted as active sites for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction and the $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ ions were reduced to $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ in the presence of TEOA. After $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the material produced $8.14\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}0^{-}$ without significant degradation of the MOF’s crystalline structure. Using a similar strategy, the photocatalytic activity of a porphyrin-based MOF (PCN-222), greatly extended the light harvesting capability of ${\bf N H}_{2}–\bf M I L-125$ such that it could absorb light from the entire visible spectrum (Fig. 19).353 PCN-222 adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adeptly $2.6\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm) and electron–hole recombination in the material was dramatically suppressed as the photoelectrons were generated by electron transfer from the organic linkers to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ –oxo clusters. Such suppression of electron–hole recombination clearly demonstrates a potential advantage of MOF application to photocatalytic redox reactions. All of these advantages led to a considerable yield of formate $(30\mathrm{\\textmumol}$ in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ ) as the product of photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
Time-resolved photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy and ultrafast transient absorption spectroscopy confirmed that an extremely long-lived electron trap state existed in PCN-222. A photosensitizing MOF featuring dinuclear ${\mathrm{Eu}}({\mathrm{m}})_{2}$ clusters and ${\mathrm{Ru}}({\mathrm{phen}})_{3}$ -derived organic linkers for visible-light-driven $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction was also reported.354 Calculations and characterization by ultrafast transient absorption, and EPR spectroscopy indicated that, upon visible light irradiation, the $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{phen}})_{3}$ linkers were excited to triplet MLCT excited states, initiating electron injection into the Eu SBUs, which then became catalytically active. In this catalytic system, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was converted to formate with a fantastic rate of $321.9~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ mmol $\mathbf{MOF}^{-1}$ , far exceeding many previous MOF catalysts under similar conditions.
In some cases, both the SBUs and organic linkers of MOFs can be simultaneously photoexcited, leading to dual photocatalytic excitation pathways in a single material.358,359 This phenomenon was reported in some amine-functionalized MOFs including $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\$ (Fe), $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–53(\mathrm{Fe})$ , and ${\bf N H}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–88\mathbf{B}(\mathrm{Fe}).^{3:}$ 8 Upon exposure to visible light, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC linkers were excited followed by electron transfer to the Fe–oxo clusters in a process analogous to the one detailed above. In addition, the Fe–oxo SBUs were directly excited, greatly enhancing the MOFs’ photocatalytic activities (Fig. 20). Without amine functionalities, a zirconium-based MOF was synthesized using an anthracenebased organic linker.359 The linker was an effective visible light harvester, transferring photogenerated electrons to the Zr–oxo SBU by LMCT. Meanwhile, photoinduced charges were generated on the linker by radical formation. The dual excitation routes simultaneously contributed to the photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction activity, yielding formate and boosting the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion efficiency.
It should be noted that each photocatalytic MOF system detailed above was synthesized in a traditional, one-pot, solvothermal manner. As an alternative, post-synthetic modification (PSM) can expand the scope of available materials to achieve effective photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.356,357,360–364 For instance, a metalloporphyrin zirconium-based MOF, MOF-525-Co, was synthesized by post-synthetic metalation of the porphyrin rings in MOF-525.360 Upon irradiation with visible light, MOF-525-Co exhibited a drastically enhanced activity compared to that of pristine MOF-525. Photoluminescent (PL) quenching measurements of MOF-525-Co demonstrated that photogenerated excitons could directionally migrate from the organic porphyrin backbone to the core (i.e. the catalytic Co center). This extended the lifetime of the photoelectrons and allowed them to react with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in a reductive fashion. In another report, two Ru complexes, $\operatorname{Ru}(\mathbf{CO})_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{Ru(bpy)_{2}C l_{2}}$ , were tethered to the open $\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N}^{\prime}$ -chelating sites in MOF-253.357 In this case, $\operatorname{Ru}(\mathbf{CO})_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{Ru(bpy)_{2}C l_{2}}$ served as the active site and photosensitizer, respectively and the total TON of the resultant catalyst was 55 after $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ of visible light irradiation, about ten times higher than that of the homogenous $\operatorname{Ru}(\mathbf{CO})_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ catalyst.
These strategies are clearly powerful, but they are marred by the fact that many organic linkers can not be easily functionalized using PSM. To address this, postsynthetic linker exchange was employed to synthesize a photoactive UiO-66 variant.361 In the report, the BDC linkers of UiO-66 were exchanged with their catechol-functionalized counterparts (catbdc). The catechol groups were subsequently metalated with $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ and $\mathbf{Ga}^{3+}$ , leading to the formation of metal-monocatecholato species within UiO-66. With the aid of 1-benzyl-1,4-dihydronicotinamide (BNAH) and TEOA, both functionalized UiO-66 frameworks displayed decent catalytic activity (6.14–11.22 TON after $^{13\mathrm{~h~}}$ ) for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction to formic acid. The $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ SBU surface of a twodimensional MOF, $\mathbf{Hf}_{12}\mathbf{-R}\mathbf{u}$ , with $\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{bpy})(\mathbf{CO})_{3}\mathbf{X}$ $[\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Re}$ and $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{Cl}$ or $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ and $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}\dot{}$ ) was modified by carboxylate exchange to afford two photosensitizing metal–organic layers (MOLs, ${\bf H}{\bf f}_{12}{\bf-R u-R e}$ and $\mathbf{Hf}_{12}\mathbf{-Ru-Mn})$ for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction (Fig. 21).362 The MOL combined $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ photosensitizers and $\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{bpy})(\mathbf{CO})_{3}\mathbf{X}$ catalysts, in order to target synergistic effects between the two species. In particular, the proximity of the MOL skeleton to the capping molecules $\left(1{-}2~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ facilitated electron transfer from the reduced photosensitizer $([\mathsf{R u-}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{+})$ to the catalytic centers $\mathrm{({\bfM}^{I}(b p y)(C O)_{3}X}$ $\mathbf{\left(M\right)}=\mathbf{\left.Re\right.}$ , $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{Cl}$ or $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , $\mathbf{X}\ =\ \mathbf{B}\mathbf{r})$ ), leading to high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction TONs of 3849 and 1347, respectively, after $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ of visible light irradiation. Remarkably, when irradiated with natural sunlight, ${\bf H}{\bf f}_{12}{\bf-R u-R e}$ catalyzed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction with extraordinary efficiency, achieving a TON of 670 in $^\mathrm{~6~h~}$ . All of these catalytic performances were evaluated by several runs with low standard deviation, indicating the reliability of catalytic activities. In addition to the organic linkers, metal atoms in MOF SBUs can also be substituted. For example, a Ti-substituted $\mathrm{{NH_{2}\mathrm{{-UiO}\mathrm{{-66}\mathrm{{(Zr/Ti)}}}}}}$ was synthesized and used as a visible-light-responsive photocatalyst for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.363 After metal substitution, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and the photocatalytic formate yield were improved compared to $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ UiO-66. In contrast to the simple LMCT mechanism postulated to occur in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66, the authors speculated that the excited $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -BDC linkers of $\mathrm{NH_{2}\mathrm{-UiO\mathrm{-}66(Z r/T i)}}$ would be inclined to transfer electrons to the Ti ions in the SBUs of $\mathrm{(Ti^{3+}/Z r^{4+})_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ and the reduced $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ functioned as electron mediators donating electrons to $\mathrm{Zr^{4}}$ .
In addition, MOFs can behave solely as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction cocatalysts, achieving high photocatalytic activities in the resultant systems.365–374 For instance, a visible-light-driven catalytic system employing ZIF-9, $\mathrm{\small{[Ru(bpy)_{3}]C l_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}}$ and TEOA as cocatalyst, photosensitizer and electron donor, respectively, was applied to the photosplitting of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .365 Upon visible-light irradiation for $30\mathrm{min}$ , the catalyst produced $71.7\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ of CO and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ based on the synergetic effects between cobalt and benzimidazolate moieties in ZIF-9. This activity surpassed those of many other MOFs such as Co-MOF-74 $\left(23.8\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\right)$ , Mn-MOF-74 $(5.5~\mathrm{\textmumol})$ , ZIF-8 $\left(5.6\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\right)$ and ${\bf N H}_{2}–\bf{U i O}–66$ $(4.3~\upmu\mathrm{mol})$ , indicating the unique ability of Co-ZIF-9 to photoreduce $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .
3.3.2. MOF composites for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Despite the fact that MOFs are appealing for photocatalysis, numerous MOFs have sufficiently large bandgaps such that they only absorb UV light and cannot be directly used for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction when irradiated with visible or longer-wavelength light. To address this challenge, metal complexes such as $\mathrm{\bigl[Ru(bpy)_{3}\bigr]C l_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ can be added to the catalytic systems to serve as photosensitizers.365–374 Nevertheless, given the high cost and homogeneous nature of these noble metal complexes, a myriad of solid materials including inorganic semiconductors and metal NPs have been employed to replace them in conventional systems.375–396 Inorganic semiconductor materials incorporated in MOFs act as photosensitizers and excite the frameworks upon exposure to light.376–378,385–389 In turn, certain MOFs can effectively suppress the recombination of electron–hole pairs by transferring photoexcited electrons from the semiconductor materials to the MOFs and/or adsorbing the dissolved $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the reaction system, significantly improving the catalytic efficiencies of the given semiconductor.375–378,385–398
A semiconductor–MOF composite was designed for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction by growing ZIF-8 nanoparticles on $\mathrm{Zn_{2}G e O_{4}}$ nanorods.375 The photocatalytic activity of the resultant $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction was investigated in aqueous solution. Under light irradiation for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ , evolution of the product (MeOH) was enhanced by $62\%$ in comparison to the pure $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}$ . The authors speculated that this was caused by the increased $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capacity and light response of the catalyst after the introduction of ZIF-8. By coating of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ onto the HKUST-1 microcrystals, a core–shell structured hybrid photocatalyst, HKUST- $\mathbf{1}@\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ , was made.376 Given that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is not able to harvest visible light HKUST- $\mathbf{1}(\mathbf{\hat{a}}\mathbf{UiO}_{2}$ was illuminated with UV light and exhibited a catalytic efficiency over five times greater than that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , with high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity. Ultrafast spectroscopy demonstrated that the photogenerated electrons were transferred from the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell to the HKUST-1 core. This enhanced the charge-separation efficiency of the semiconductor and supplied adequate electrons to the adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules. All these factors facilitated highly selective conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ by an eight-electron process. Nanosheets were also incorporated with MOFs in an electrostatic self-assembly approach that integrated positively-charged UiO-66 crystals with negatively-charged nanosized carbon nitride nanosheets (CNNS).377 The resultant material was a UiO-66/CNNS heterogeneous photocatalyst. The CO evolution rate afforded by UiO-66/CNNS was $9.9\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g_{CN}}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ under light irradiation for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , over three times greater than that of CNNS $\left(2.9\upmu\mathrm{mol}\up g_{\mathrm{CN}}{}^{-1}\thinspace\mathrm{h}^{-1}\right)$ . The authors ascribed the improvement of photocatalytic performance to the electron transfer from the photoexcited CNNS to UiO-66 and effective inhibition of the electron–hole pair recombination in the CNNS, ultimately offering long-lived electrons for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. More recently, this self-assembly method was applied to the combination of BIF-20 with graphitic carbon nitride $\left({\bf g}{\bf-}{\bf C}_{3}{\bf N}_{4}\right)$ to prepare a $\mathbf{BIF}–20@\mathbf{g}–\mathbf{C}_{3}\mathbf{N}_{4}$ nanosheet photocatalyst.378 When the surface of BIF-20 was loaded with $20\%\mathrm{g}–\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , the resultant composite exhibited the highest catalytic performance $\mathrm{(CH_{4}}$ evolution rate of $15.524\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and CO evolution rate of $53.869~\mathrm{\upmumol~g^{-1}~h^{-1}})$ , outperforming the $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheet, bulk $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , and ZIF- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf8}({\bf\widehat{a}}){\bf g}-{\bf C}_{3}{\bf N}_{4}$ nanosheet. First-principles simulations indicated that the B–H bonds in BIF-20 functioned not only as electron capture centers to prevent the recombination of electron–hole pairs but also activated the adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules in BIF-20, greatly facilitating the photoreduction process. More recently, a ternary CdS/UiO-bpy/Co composite was synthesized by combining inorganic semiconductors (CdS) and molecular redox catalysts (Co complex) in a MOF.379 Under visible light irradiation, the Co complex accepted electrons from the CdS photosensitizer and subsequently photochemically reduced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to CO. Additionally, in the composites, CdS NPs ( $\sim10~\mathrm{nm})$ were obtained due to the coordination of bpydc with $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ , eventually affording an high CO evolution rate of $235\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with a desirable selectivity $(>85\%)$ . The authors attributed the high catalytic performance to the promoted separation and migration of photo-induced charge carriers, enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity (compared with pure CdS) as well as the abundant active sites for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
As a matter of fact, MOFs can also serve as photosensitizers in some semiconductor–MOF composites.380–382 A prime example is found in a series of $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}_{}\mathrm{@NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 composites.380 Under visible-light irradiation, both $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}S$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 were excited, generating electron–hole pairs. Since the LUMO potential of $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 ( $_{(-0.60\ \mathrm{eV}}$ vs. NHE) is more negative than the conduction band (CB) potential of $\mathrm{Cd_{0.2}Z n_{0.8}S\left(-0.56e V\right.}$ vs. NHE), the photogenerated electrons can transfer from the LUMO of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 to the CB of $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}$ , suppressing the recombination of electron–hole pairs and prolonging the lifetime of photoinduced charge carriers. This ultimately accelerated the photocatalytic conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ up to $6.8~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under visible-light irradiation, over 3 times higher than that of pure $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S},$ . Since the LUMO potential of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 is more negative than the CB potential of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(-0.28~eV}$ vs. NHE), a Type II heterojunction was formed in a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ UiO-66 composite.381 By transferring photogenerated electrons from $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the electron–hole separation in $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}.$ UiO-66 was promoted and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was reduced to CO by $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , with a superior CO evolution rate as compared to the pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ .
photocatalytic conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to CO compared to pristine ${\bf R e}_{3}–\mathbf{MOF}$ , with long-term catalytic stability maintained up to $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
To summarize, conventional strategies applied to make MOF composite photocatalysts include: (1) combination of MOFs with semiconductors such as $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The intimate interfacial contact between semiconductors and MOFs results in the formation of heterojunctions, while the MOFs act as cocatalysts. This synergy induces efficient, persistent electron–hole separation and thus improves photocatalytic efficiency. (2) Stabilization of cocatalysts such as $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}$ by MOFs. The conjugated organic linkers in MOFs can behave as molecular antennae to be photoexcited. The photoelectrons then transfer to the cocatalyst for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. (3) Immobilization of photoactive metal nanoparticles into MOFs. Such components harvest solar energy, exciting and then transferring electrons to active sites in the MOF(s) for subsequent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
3.3.3. MOF derivatives for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. In an analogous fashion to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture, MOF pyrolysis can be applied to the synthesis of materials for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.399–403 To various extents, these catalysts inherit the pore characteristics of their MOF templates. This not only benefits the adsorption and subsequent concentration of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , but can also create new catalytically active sites.
A MOF-derived $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ material was made by directly pyrolyzing ${\bf N H}_{2}{\bf-M I L-}125$ in an oxygen atmosphere and applied to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction.399 When the precursor $\left({\bf N H}_{2}{\bf-M I L}{\bf-}125\right)$ was replaced by ${\bf N H}_{2}{\bf-M I L}{\bf-}125$ doped with gold NPs (GNPs), a dramatic increase in $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ yield was observed due to the visible light absorption of gold surface plasmon and/or the electron storage capacities of GNPs, and the minimal $\mathbf{GNP/TiO}_{2}$ lattice defects, which effectively inhibited electron–hole recombination. Additionally, core–shell ZIF- ${8}\textcircled{\mathrm{2}}\mathrm{ZIF-6}7$ crystals were employed as precursors for the synthesis of a porous $\mathrm{ZnO@Co_{3}O_{4}}$ composite.400 Interestingly, unlike the unstable $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ evolution observed in ZIF-8-derived $z\mathrm{no}$ , the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ evolution increased steadily during the entire reaction involving $\mathbf{ZnO}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})$ $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ . This was attributed to suppressed ZnO photocorrosion after embedding the $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ co-catalyst on the surface.
Some structural features of MOF-derived materials (e.g. hollowness) can reduce bulk-to-surface diffusion length (accelerating electron–hole separation) and provide high surface area in order to boost $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and substrate/product transfer.401–404
These features play significant roles in enhancing a given MOF derivative’s photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction activity. Using MIL-68(In) as precursor, a series of sandwich-like $\mathrm{ZnIn_{2}S_{4}–I n_{2}O_{3}}$ photocatalysts with hierarchical tubular heterostructures were generated by growing $\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ on both surfaces of the $\mathbf{In}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ microtubes (Fig. 23).401 The highest CO evolution rate of $\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\mathrm{-In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ was $3075\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was further confirmed by repeated experiments. The authors attributed this to the promoted transport of photoinduced charges and accelerated electron– hole separation from the double-heterojunction formed by the sandwich-like structure.
Again, MOF derivatives do not have to be synthesized by high-temperature pyrolysis. Indeed, the same team demonstrated the construction of hierarchical $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ nanotubes by converting MIL-68(In) to hierarchical $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ nanotubes through a liquid phase sulfidation process with subsequent cation exchange.404 The hierarchical heterostructured tubes were capable of separating and migrating photoinduced charge carriers, improving the material’s $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, and providing abundant catalytically active sites. The resultant material exhibited a decent CO production rate $\left(825\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ in the presence of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}$ and TEOA, approximately 12 times higher than that of pure $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ .
## 3.4. Electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction
Electrochemical carbon dioxide reduction reactions $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})$ can be accomplished under mild conditions, putting them in stark contrast with many other transformations. Furthermore, the energy utilized to carry out these reactions can be obtained from renewable energy sources. Generally, electrochemical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction occurs by proton abstraction from solvent molecule(s), yielding various fuels and value-added chemicals.
3.4.1. MOFs for electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Extensive efforts have been devoted to the development of homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ . Outstanding challenges remain, such as low catalytic efficiency, undesirable selectivity, and poor long-term stability. Fortunately, MOFs can incorporate redox-active sites, concentrate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , and even facilitate uniform proton transport to catalytic sites. In this section we give examples of how these properties allow MOFs to improve upon previous attempts to affect $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ reactions.
Inspired by the unique catalytic properties of metallic Cu for electrochemical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction, select proton-conductive, Cu-based, MOFs have been applied as electrochemical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ catalysts.405–410 As an example, a copper rubeanate MOF was used for highly selective conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to HCOOH.405 Since HCOOH is easily produced at relatively weak $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, the final selectivity of HCOOH using this MOF was $98\%$ , significantly greater than that of metallic Cu $(\sim38\%)$ , with a current efficiency of $30\%$ from $-1.6$ to $-1.2\mathrm{V}$ vs. SHE, due to its weak $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption behavior. Current efficiency (also called faradaic efficiency) which refers to the percentage of charge from an electrochemical process that is used to generate a specific product is a critical parameter used to evaluate the applicability of a given electrode. As a typical copper-based MOF, HKUST-1 has been deposited on different electrodes to serve as an electrocatalyst.406–409 In these reports, oxalic acid, alcohols, and hydrocarbons have been produced. More recently, the paddle-wheel Cu dimer SBUs of HKUST-1 were desymmetrized to target multiple-carbon product generation (Fig. 24).409 By simply heating the material to $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , adjacent benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate (BTC) moieties were separated from the Cu dimer, decreasing the Cu–Cu coordination number (CN) in HKUST-1 as confirmed by extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectroscopy. When the calcination time was increased to $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , the Cu–Cu CN reduced to $9.5\pm0.9$ while the framework maintained its structural integrity and crystallinity. The $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4}$ faradaic efficiency (FE) in this distorted HKUST-1 variant was $45\%$ with a current density of $262~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $-1.07\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ vs. RHE, whereas the best $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4}$ FE was only $\sim10\%$ in pristine HKUST-1, yielding a range of products. The experiment were reproducible with small data deviation, indicating the reliability.
To produce another MOF-based electrocatalyst, Zn-MOFs were deposited on carbon paper (CP), yielding Zn-MOF/CP electrodes by an electrophoretic deposition (EPD) method.
These electrodes were used to convert $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ using ionic liquid (IL) electrolytes.411 Fluorine-containing, imidazoliumbased ILs can not only absorb and activate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules but also promote the transformation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ intermediate. The highest electrochemical activity $(80.1\pm6.6\%$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ FE) was obtained with a current density of $3.1\pm\:0.5\:\mathrm{\mA}\:\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at an applied potential of $-2.2\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. ${\bf\cal A}{\bf g}/{\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ over $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ based on the combination of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Zn-MOF/CP}$ cathode and the IL $\mathrm{\left(BmimBF_{4,}\right.}$ . Four zinc-based ZIFs, including ZIF-7, ZIF-8, ZIF-108, and SIM-1 were studied for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ activity in an aqueous electrolyte.412 In a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}.$ saturated $0.25\mathrm{~M~K_{2}S O_{4}}$ solution $\mathrm{(pH}=4.2\mathrm{)}$ , the best CO faradaic efficiency was $81.0\%$ at $-1.1\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE over ZIF-8, and ZIF-108 manifested the highest CO current density $(12.8\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at $-1.3\mathrm{V}\nu s$ . RHE). Imidazolate ligands coordinated with the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ center was proved to be the active sites in ZIFs for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ by in situ X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) spectra and DFT calculations.
Incorporating redox-active organic linkers into MOFs can also yield materials with enhanced catalytic CO2RR performance.413–416 A thin film MOF electrode was made by depositing alumina ( $\mathrm{~\bar{~}{5}~n m}$ thickness) onto conductive carbon disk electrodes using atomic-layer deposition (ALD) and subsequently converting the alumina films to Al-TCPP-Co (Fig. 25a).413 A CO selectivity over $76\%$ was achieved by the resultant electrode with a per-site TON of 1400 and extremely small deviations were found upon repetitions of the experiment. The catalyst remained stable for up to $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ . In situ spectroelectrochemical measurements demonstrated that the porphyrinic $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ were reduced to Co(I) during electrocatalysis. Liquid-phase epitaxy has also been demonstrated to be a valid approach for fabricating homogeneous and highly orientated MOF thin films of controllable thicknesses. For example, highly oriented surface grafted MOF (SURMOF) thin films were grown on fluorine doped tin oxide (FTO) glass electrodes using a liquid-phase epitaxy process.414 The $\mathrm{Re(CO)}_{3}(\mathrm{bpydc)Cl}$ linkers in the SURMOF electrocatalytically reduced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with a FE up to $93\pm5\%$ at a $-1.6{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. NHE potential, far exceeding that of the homogeneous $\mathrm{Re(CO)_{3}(b p y d c)C l}$ catalyst. Using a post-synthetically metalated Fe-porphyrinbased MOF-525 thin film electrode on FTO glass, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ was reduced to CO (Fig. 25b).415 Controlled potential electrolysis (CPE) experiments (at $-1.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. NHE) showed that the current density increased to $5.9\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ and the FEs of CO and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ were $\mathbf{41\pm8}$ and $60\pm4\%$ , respectively, after $\sim4\mathrm{~h~}$ of electrolysis, which implied that the total faradaic efficiency $\left(\mathbf{CO}+\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)$ of this catalytic system was $\sim100\%$ .
All of the abovementioned cases demonstrate that MOFs are a superb platform to immobilize redox-active moieties, yielding heterogeneous catalysts for effective and selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ . However, due to the poor electrical conductivities of most MOFs, at this stage, working electrodes for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ must be fabricated by the deposition of MOF nanocrystals on various conductive substrates such as CP, FTO glass, gas diffusion electrodes, etc.405–419
3.4.2. Carbon materials derived from MOFs for electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. MOFs can also be used to form carbon-based MOF derivatives for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ . These compounds usually possess excellent stabilities and high electrical conductivities, making them powerful electrocatalysts. Moreover, the well-dispersed active sites are confined in the resultant carbon matrix. This can dramatically improve the catalytic activities and selectivities of the associated reactions. Additionally, the resultant porous structures provide a means of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ enrichment, greatly affecting $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ chemical transformations by increasing local concentrations.
As an example, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ electrochemical conversion capabilities of oxide-derived $\mathrm{Cu\it/}$ /carbons $\mathbf{\left(ODCu/C\right)}$ fabricated by carbonization of HKUST-1 were evaluated.420 The highest methanol yield was obtained using the catalyst OD $\mathrm{\bfCu/C^{\mathrm{-1000}}}$ , which produced $12.4~\mathrm{{\mg}~L^{-1}~h^{-1}}$ at $-0.3\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE. Additionally, and the maximum ethanol production rate ( $\mathbf{\bar{1}}3.4\ \mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}~h^{-1}})$ was observed at $-0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE. This was attributed to synergistic effects between the highly dispersed Cu and porous carbon matrix. Recently, ZIF-8 was also utilized as a precursor in the preparation of nitrogen-doped porous carbons.421 The resultant materials exhibited excellent electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to CO reduction with a CO FE of $\sim78\%$ at $-0.93{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE on account of the presence of abundant pyridinic-N and quaternary-N species. Both the pyridinic-N and quaternary-N species in the carbon matrix can promote the transfer of proton–electron pairs to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , lowering the energy barrier for the production of the intermediate $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{COOH^{\star}})$ for CO formation. A series of nitrogen-doped carbons containing atomically dispersed Co sites was synthesized by annealing bimetallic $\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{Zn}$ ZIFs at different temperatures (Fig. 26).422 Interestingly, the N coordination number of Co sites could be controlled by varying the temperature at which the pyrolysis was performed. The catalyst containing $\mathbf{Co-N}_{2}$ active sites was more capable of activating $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , leading to the generation of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ intermediate. Therefore, this catalyst exhibited the highest electrocatalytic performance with a CO FE and current density of $94\%$ and $18.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, at an overpotential of $520~\mathrm{mV}$ , among the investigated samples. Notably, the CO TOF over this Co catalyst with the lowest N coordination number was up to $182000~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , outperforming most reported metal-based catalysts under comparable conditions. Two atomically dispersed Fe and Co catalysts (Fe–N–C and Co–N–C), were synthesized using bimetallic $\mathrm{Fe/Zn}$ and $\mathbf{Co}/\$ Zn ZIFs, separately, as precursors.423 In comparison to the optimized catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Co-C-N}$ (CO FE of $45\%$ at an overpotential of 0.48 V), Fe–N–C displayed a higher CO FE of ${>}93\%$ with a current density of $-2.8~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a low overpotential of $0.47\mathrm{~V~}$ . The first principle calculations indicated that the edge-hosted $\bf{M}\mathrm{-}\bf{N}_{2+2}\mathrm{-}\bf{C}_{8}$ moieties bridged by two adjacent armchair-like graphitic layers were more active than the bulk-hosted $\bf{M}\mathrm{{-}\mathbf{N}_{4}\mathrm{{-}\mathbf{C}_{10}}}$ moieties embedded in the graphitic layers with respect to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ . Particularly, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ is more favorable than hydrogen evolution on the $\mathrm{Fe-N}_{2+2}{-}\mathrm{C}_{8}$ sites of the Fe–N–C catalyst, leading to the reported remarkable CO FE.
In contrast to the direct thermal decomposition of MOFs, a ZIF-assisted strategy was employed to synthesize Ni single atoms distributed in a nitrogen-doped porous carbon (Ni SAs/N–C) matrix. This was accomplished by exchanging ions between the Zn SBUs and the adsorbed Ni salts in the pores of ZIF-8.424 Compared with Ni NPs and Ni foam, the obtained Ni SAs/N–C catalyst was able to selectively reduce $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with an excellent current density and CO FE of $10.48~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $71.9\%$ , respectively, at an overpotential of $0.89\mathrm{~V~}_{;}$ indicative of the superiority of single atom catalysts for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}.$ . Furthermore, no obvious decline of CO FE and current density was observed during $60\mathrm{~h~}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ electroreduction at a potential of $-1.0{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE, demonstrating the high electrocatalytic stability of this single-atom catalyst. The surface of ZIF-8 was functionalized by taking advantage of the coordination interactions between Zn nodes and ammonium ferric citrate (AFC).425 Upon pyrolysis, isolated iron–nitrogen (Fe–N) sites were formed on the surface of the resulting carbon matrix. These highly exposed Fe–N sites endowed the resultant catalyst (C-AFC $\copyright$ ZIF-8) with an optimal faradaic efficiency for CO production of $93.0\%$ at an overpotential of $-0.43{\mathrm{~V~}}$ .
In order to further improve the electrocatalytic performance of carbon materials derived from MOFs for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ , a hybrid material, composed of ZIFs and multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) loaded with furfuryl alcohol (FA) was used as a precursor, to fabricate a pyrolyzed ZIFs supported on carbon nanotube networks.426 The MWCNT support was capable of enhancing the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ mass transport and electron transport in the resultant hybrid catalyst, resulting in a fantastic CO faradaic efficiency $(\sim100\%)$ and a high current density of $7.7\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at a low overpotential of $740~\mathrm{mV}.$ . When the hybrid catalyst was doped with Fe, the overpotential was as low as $440~\mathrm{{mV}}$ with a desirable CO FE and current density of $97\%$ and $2\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively. Given the decent electrocatalytic activities of transition-metal phosphides, MoP NPs supported on In-doped porous carbon (In-PC) were made using MIL-68(In) as a carbon precursor.427 The hybrid structure, strong interactions, and cooperative effects between the nano-MoP $\left(4\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ and In-PC support of the resultant catalyst $\mathbf{\left(MoP\mathcal{Q}I n–P C\right)}$ ensured more active sites and space for efficient $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and electrolyte diffusion to the electrode. Additionally, these features allowed for faster electron transfer to the adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to form $\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ intermediates than in the bulk MoP. Accordingly, $\mathbf{MoP@In–PC}$ exhibited excellent catalytic performance for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}$ to formic acid with an outstanding faradaic efficiency and current density of $96.5\%$ and $43.8\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, by choosing ionic liquid 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium hexafluorophosphate as the electrolyte.
4. The relationship between ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture capacity of MOF-based materials and their catalytic performance for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion
After over two decades of investigation, MOFs that outperform other porous materials with respect to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity have been developed. Additionally, catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion in MOF-based materials has also been well studied. In this context, an understanding of how the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capabilities of MOF-based materials affect their catalytic conversion efficiencies is paramount. Such an understanding will guide the development of efficient catalysts for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion.
The relationship between the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacity of a given MOFbased material and its catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion efficiency is clear. This relationship is governed by the following phenomena: (1) MOF-based materials can increase the local $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ concentration around materials’ catalytically active sites by $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. In this context, MOF pores serve as ‘‘microreactors’’, providing favorable environments for reactions between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and substrates. This has led to the observation of high reaction yields and selectivities in MOF-based materials; (2) some functional groups in MOFs enhance their $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake and activate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules and/or other reactants, further improving MOFs’ catalytic efficiencies;428 (3) the high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption selectivities of MOF-based materials can guarantee high catalytic performance even in mixed-gas environments with low $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ concentrations.109,286,313,367,369,374
To demonstrate the culmination of these phenomena, we will present a couple of examples. One is found in a ${\bf U i O-}67{\bf-R u}^{\mathrm{II}}$ composite (UiO-67/RuCO). This material was prepared by incorporating a photoactive $\mathbf{{R}}\mathbf{{u}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{{-CO}}$ complex $\mathrm{([Ru^{H}(b p y)(t e r p y)(C O)](P F_{6})_{2})}$ into UiO-67 using a facile PSE method (Fig. 27a).369 Although the total TON produced by the homogeneous $\mathrm{{Ru}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}.$ –CO complex catalyst was slightly higher than that of the composite, the photocatalytic activity of the molecular catalyst was drastically reduced as the partial pressure of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ decreased (Fig. 27b). This indicated that catalysis was heavily dependent on the concentration of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in the reaction mixture. In stark contrast, UiO-67/RuCO displayed almost the same catalytic performance even under a $5\%$ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ atmosphere. This surpassed both the homogeneous catalyst and a physical mixture of UiO-67 and the $\mathrm{{Ru}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ –CO complex, and the results demonstrated that the UiO-67/RuCO composite could enrich gas mixtures containing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and take advantage of synergy between the adsorptive sites and the catalysts’ active sites. Similarly, the catalytic performance of an IL-MOF composite (polyILs@MIL-101) for cycloaddition of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with ECH was maintained in a wide $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure range from $0.5\substack{-1.5}$ bar, whereas the catalytic activity of the pure IL significantly declined once the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure was reduced to 0.5 bar.313 More recently, three isostructural MOFs (MAF-X27-Cl, MAF-X27-OH and MOF-74-Co) were used for photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction.367 Interestingly, when the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ partial pressure was reduced to 0.1 atm, only MAF-X27-OH exhibited a high CO TOF of $23\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ with an unaltered CO selectivity $(97.2\%)$ compared to the TOF at 1 atm $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ $(28\ \times\ 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ (Fig. 27c). Periodic density functional theory (PDFT) simulations revealed that the $\scriptstyle{\upmu}-\mathrm{OH}$ coordinated on the open Co sites of MAF-X27-OH stabilized the $\mathbf{Co-CO_{2}}$ adduct by strong hydrogen bonding and promoted $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Furthermore, it was observed that MAF-X27-OH bound $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ more strongly than $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ (Fig. 27d). This suppressed $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ (the byproduct) production and ensured high CO selectivity. A similar phenomenon was observed in monolayers of a Ni metal–organic framework (Ni MOLs).374 In simulated flue-gas $\left(10\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ , the photocatalytic activity and selectivity of Ni MOLs was nearly equivalent to those obtained in pure $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ due to the stronger adsorption energy of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\left(-200.11\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ on Ni MOLs than $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\left(-62.47\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ . As a final example, a $\mathbf{Cu(\mathrm{II})-M O F}$ with high density of LASs and LBSs exhibited remarkable $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ volumetric uptake $\left(171\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\right.$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm) and excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity (51 for the $15/$ $85~\mathrm{v/v}$ mixture at 1 atm) due to the cooperation of multiple active sites.109 The MOF, FJI-H14, was then employed to catalyze $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions using 1 atm simulated post-combustion flue gas $\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}:\mathbf{N}_{2}=15:85\right)$ ) as a feedstock. The material exhibited a desirable product yield $(86\%)$ , outperforming the copper salt $(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2})$ , the physical mixture of the copper salt $\big(\mathrm{Cu}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{2}\big)$ and the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{a}$ linker, as well as a typical $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ -based MOF (HKUST-1) (Fig. 27e), again providing evidence for the synergistic effects between LASs and LBSs.
## 5. Conclusions and perspectives
The development of MOF-based materials has opened a door leading to a new set of promising adsorbents and catalysts for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and conversion. Keys to the successful implementation of MOFs as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbents lie in their chemical tunabilities that improve interactions with the gaseous molecule and even other substrates. These interactions are mostly between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and open metal sites, Lewis basic sites, polar functional groups, and/or micropores. Exploration of these interactions has precipitated magnificent adsorption capacities and selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture in the presence of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , among other gases. Furthermore, given the challenges involved with the introduction of certain functional groups into MOFs by de novo syntheses, various post-synthetic strategies, such as post-synthetic modification and the construction of MOF composites, have been developed. Large-scale application of MOFs to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture remains subject to research to address further improvements of selectivity in the presence of water or acidic gases. It is incumbent upon researchers to choose the appropriate MOFs for the desired performance.
Many classes of reactions for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion have been explored over MOFs, mainly including $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ organic transformation, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation, photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Beyond that, MOFs can be combined with other materials or used as precursors to synthesize MOF composites and MOF derivatives, respectively. Generally, the catalytic efficiencies of MOF-based materials are favorable. This can be attributed to the following factors: (a) the relatively high surface areas and porous structures stabilize the catalytically active moieties and prevent deactivation while guaranteeing mass transfer and electron migration; (b) the synergetic effects from multiple functionalities can activate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and/or substrate molecules, facilitating $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion; (c) based on their specific compositions, MOF composites and derivatives exhibit enhanced electrical conductivities, visible-light absorbance, and/or stabilities further expanding potential applications of catalysts.
These MOF-based materials would not be efficient catalysts, however, if it were not for their high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capacities. Thus, the relationship between the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capacity of MOF-based materials and their catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion performance is critical. In this review, we have highlighted this importance in three manners: (a) in contrast to homogeneous catalysts, the local $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ concentration at catalytically active sites can be vastly increased by the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capabilities of MOFbased materials, boosting reaction yields and selectivities; (b) certain functional groups in MOFs not only improve the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities but also activate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules and/or other reactants, further enhancing the catalytic efficiencies of MOF-based materials; (c) the high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption selectivities of MOF-based materials can endow them with high catalytic activities and selectivities even in mixed gas environments with low $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ concentrations or partial pressures. Therefore, in the future, the synthesis of MOF-based materials with outstanding $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities and selectivities must be tied in with the development of catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion.
Although dramatic advances have been made in the last ten years, this field is still in its infancy. This is especially true in regards to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation, photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and electrocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Many open issues remain that should be addressed as MOF-based $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion catalysts undergo further development. First, a plethora of reported catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion reactions are carried out under harsh conditions. For example, some organic transformations and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction reactions are performed in alkaline environments,3 and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation is mainly carried out at high temperatures. Fortunately, great progress on this topic has been achieved in recent years. Also, as we have demonstrated, most MOF-based catalytic systems still require the presence of co-catalysts or electron sacrificial agents. Although these compounds significantly boost the performance of MOF-based catalysts, they can also serve to pollute the environment or consume/sacrifice active sites, making the materials less efficient. Therefore, it is important that future studies focus on integrating these active species into the frameworks or mitigating the need for additional reagents whatsoever. Unfortunately, the catalytic efficiencies of many MOF-based materials featuring mainly micropores are restricted by mass transfer. To address this issue, 2D MOFs and hierarchically porous MOFs might be worthy of further investigation. As for the stability of materials, cycling performance tests might not be sufficient to prove that the catalysts are permanently stable.
As the field moves toward the industrial application phase, the reproducibility of reported catalytic activities and long-term stability of MOF-based catalysts should be evaluated more thoroughly. Finally, while MOFs with well-defined structures are beneficial when it comes to mechanistic understanding, advanced characterization and multi-dimensional in operando reactions are still important. Therefore, in order to understand the reaction mechanisms involved with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion in MOFbased catalysts and further push to practical application in the future, it is likely that this ongoing research will require collaboration between scientists from different disciplines including theoretical, synthetic, and physical chemistry, chemical engineering, and physics.
MOF-based materials are already showing great promise as the next-generation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and conversion systems. That is evidenced not only by the examples we detailed in this review, but also in the speed with which the field is advancing. We foresee additional rapid growth as there are numerous opportunities to design, synthesize, characterize, and utilize MOF-based materials for this set of critical applications.
